\cparagraph{Equal shares improves voters' geographic representation, especially for city peripheries} Figure~\ref{fig:results}b illustrates that the method of equal shares results in 5 districts (27.77\%) with more winning projects compared to the majoritarian standard method, while no district receives a lower number of projects. This particularly benefits the periphery of the city that would otherwise be under-represented. Moreover, Figure~\ref{fig:results}c measures the relative number of winning/proposed projects in each district. Using the method of equal shares, the spatial fairness increases by 44\% when measured by the reduction in the index of dispersion, which demonstrates that more proposed project win in each district. These results confirm that the periphery of Aarau is better represented, both in terms of the voter preferences and the number of proposed project ideas selected. 

\cparagraph{The method of equal shares represents voters better} Figure~\ref{fig:representation}a shows the share of voters for different cumulative levels of the mean representation of their preferences. Representation is measured by the relative number of projects that are preferred by a voter and win. Comparing the standard method of 345 past participatory budgeting processes~\cite{Faliszewski2023,pabulib} with the outcomes for the equal shares method in Aarau, we observe 3.7\% more voters with higher representation levels, in particular for lower levels of cumulative representation. This means that equal shares better preserves the representation of the population even for under-represented voters, whose preferences do not align well with the voting outcomes. Specifically, for the participatory budgeting process in Aarau, the voters' representation is 19\% higher under equal shares compared to the standard method and 14.5\% higher on average compared to 345 earlier participatory budgeting processes. 

