\cparagraph{Equal shares encourages proposed projects that are novel and win} Figure~\ref{fig:representation}b illustrates the share of the proposed project categories of participatory budgeting in Aarau compared to the projects observed in earlier participatory budgeting processes~\cite{Faliszewski2023,pabulib}. We find a significant change in the nature of the proposed projects: welfare and health projects, which were usually under-represented, are more prominently featured, while public transit projects are less prominent. This shift is determined by the costs of the projects belonging in the two categories: the average cost of public transit projects in the participatory budgeting process of Aarau amounts to 8\% of the overall budget, while welfare-related projects amount to 12.25\%. In earlier participatory budgeting processes, these numbers were 18.19\% and 7.52\%. Figure~\ref{fig:representation}c presents the share of the winning project categories in the participatory budgeting process of Aarau for the method of equal shares and for the majoritarian standard method. It also shows the share in earlier participatory budgeting experience that relied on the standard method. Health and sport would be disadvantaged with the standard method as it would reduce their share in the winning set by 19.13\% and 18.05\%, respectively.

\cparagraph{Broader evidence beyond the case of Aarau} To strengthen further our findings, we also highlight the case of two other participatory budgeting elections that used equal shares during 2023 in two cities of Poland: (i) Świecie and (ii) Wieliczka (``"Green Million"). In the first case that was a follow up of Aarau, 17/22 projects are selected for funding, while the majoritarian standard method would result in only 9 winning projects. The representation of voters' preferences increased from 61\% to 80\%,. The top-2 project in voters' approvals is not funded, which requires 36.6\% of the budget and is approved by 24.4\% of the population. In the second case, 30/64 projects are selected for funding, while the majoritarian standard method would result in 23 winning projects. The representation of voters preferences increased from 61\% to 71\%,. The top-2 project in voters' approvals is not funded, which requires 10\% of the budget and is approved by 8.9\% of the population. Both cases confirm the distinguishing voting outcomes of equal shares: proportional representation is achieved by funding more projects that increase the overall satisfaction with compromises on popular expensive projects. In contrast to these two elections, further novel data are collected during the participatory budgeting process of Aarau that allow us to rigorously explore the legitimacy of fair voting methods and which citizens' values explain the shift to those. 


\subsection{Overcoming the adoption barrier of fair voting improves legitimacy}\label{subsec:legitimacy-gain}

\cparagraph{Adopting a new ballot aggregation method: a rigorous evaluation} Figure~\ref{fig:legitimacy} illustrates which ballot aggregation method voters prefer and which they find fairer in the entry survey before voting and in the exit survey after voting. Section~\ref{sec:choice-ballot-aggregation} also shows additional results of which method voters find more convenient. Section~\ref{sec:equal-shares-explanation} further illustrates how the two methods are presented to voters, in particular the voting outcomes and the explanations, based on which the measurements are made. The shift from the majoritarian standard method to the method of equal shares becomes obvious by the gain in the share of voters, who \emph{prefer} and find \emph{fairer} equal shares after voting (rigorously measured on the same matched population). We also assessed which method voters found more convenient in Figure~\ref{fig:convenience}. The legitimacy of the two ballot aggregation methods is determined by measuring the satisfaction of voters with the voting outcomes of each method (even if the voter classifies as a `loser', based on different measurements), see Section~\ref{sec:legitimacy} for more information. To assess legitimacy thoroughly, the voters' gain is disaggregated further down to all 24 combinations of winners/losers and satisfied/unsatisfied voters (see Figure~\ref{fig:legitimacy_fairness_preference_heat}). We group those into three groups in Figure~\ref{fig:legitimacy} for simplicity: satisfied winners, unsatisfied losers and mixed, each consisting of 8 combinations. Three ways to measure each of the winners/losers and satisfied/unsatisfied are illustrated in Section~\ref{sec:legitimacy}.  

	\begin{figure}[!htb]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{legitimacy.pdf}
    		\caption{\textbf{In the exit survey after voting, the method of equal shares is found more preferred and fairer than the majoritarian standard ballot aggregation method. Strikingly, before voting and adopting equal shares, there is no clear preference for any of the two methods, while the standard method is found fairer. Voting outcomes themselves are sufficient for individuals to determine equal shares as their preferred and fair ballot aggregation method. The explanation of the methods does not significantly contribute to shifting the choice to equal shares.} The legitimacy of equal shares is assessed based on voters' (A) preference and perceived (B) fairness of equal shares over the standard method. The x-axis determines the difference of the voters share between those choosing equal shares over the standard method. Legitimacy is assessed based on 24 combinations of winners/losers and satisfied/unsatisfied voters that are grouped into (i) winners and satisfied, (ii) losers and unsatisfied and (iii) mixed. Winners/losers are determined in three different ways: 1st and 2nd quartile of representation score for the winning projects that are (i) non-exclusive and (ii) exclusive between the two ballot aggregation methods. (iii) The non-supporters and supporters of the top popular losing project. Satisfied/unsatisfied voters are determined by three post-voting survey questions that assess: (i) satisfaction, (ii) acceptance of outcome and (iii) accurate representation of will. }
		\label{fig:legitimacy}
	\end{figure}

\cparagraph{The adoption of equal shares exhibits a shift to be the most preferred and fairer method} The results of Figure~\ref{fig:legitimacy} show that, in the exit survey after voting as compared to the entry survey before, there is a significant gain in voters who prefer the method of equal share and find it fairer. This is particularly prominent for unsatisfied losers who choose the standard method as fairer before voting but switch to equal shares after voting (see Figure~\ref{fig:legitimacy_fairness_preference_heat}). More specifically, 54\% of the population prefers equal shares before voting, while after voting, supporters of equal shares raise to 80\% (p=0.0034). Similarly, 45\% of voters find equal shares fairer before voting and 89\% fairer after voting (p=0.028). 

\cparagraph{The method of equal shares shows stronger legitimacy} The significant legitimacy of equal shares is reflected in several ways (see also Figure~\ref{fig:legitimacy_fairness_preference_heat}): (i) Losers, determined as supporters of the top popular losing project (`Wild bees' paradise'), prefer the method of equal shares and find it fairer. (ii) Remarkably, the method of equal shares is preferred and found fairer even more among losers than among winning voters, who prefer the majoritarian standard method. (iii) The shift to equal shares after voting is even found among those who do not accept the voting outcome, which is a strong proxy of legitimacy~\cite{Weatherford1992,Johnson2006}. 

\cparagraph{When shift to equal shares is not achieved}
However, we also found some cases where more voters shifted to the majoritarian standard method after voting, namely (i) satisfied voters preferring the standard method over unsatisfied voters preferring equal shares; (ii) voters accepting the voting outcome and finding the standard method fairer over voters who do not accept the voting outcome and find equal shares fairer; (iii) unsatisfied losers (or winners), who prefer the standard method over unsatisfied winners (or losers), who choose equal shares, when winners/losers are determined by the representation and their choice for the top popular losing project (`Wild bees' paradise'). See Figure~\ref{fig:legitimacy_fairness_preference_heat} for a more disaggregated view. %Furthermore, voters who prefer the standard method over equal shares before voting mostly remained hesitant to switch to equal shares, even after the concept was explained in the exit survey (see Figure~\ref{fig:convenience}).

\cparagraph{Voting outcome itself vs. method explanation: what matters} The method explanation has an additional contribution of 5.3\% to the shift to equal shares as \textit{preferred} method, while it has an addition contribution of 5.2\% to the shift to equal share as \textit{fairer} method. The method explanation has a small but statistically significant contribution to the shift to equal shares (with only exception the mixed setting for the preferred method, p=0.07). These results demonstrate that the voting outcome itself is sufficient to establish a change. %contrary to a long-standing resistance to adopt alternative methods only because they are more complex than established ones. 
