\section{Experimental Setup}
\paragraph{Training Details}
We fine-tune four base models: bge-m3~\citep{chen-etal-2024-m3}, multilingual-e5-large, multilingual-e5-base~\citep{wang2024multilingual}, and gte-multilingual-base~\citep{zhang2024mgte} using each of the language combination datasets constructed in the previous section. The training employs GISTEmbedLoss~\citep{solatorio2024gistembed}, a contrastive loss function, with bge-m3 serving as the guide model. Comprehensive training parameters and environment details are provided in Appendix~\ref{appen_exp_setup}.
\paragraph{Evaluation Datasets \& Metrics}
To assess the retrieval performance of the trained embedding models, we utilize two Korean-English parallel retrieval datasets: Belebele~\citep{bandarkar2023belebele} and StrategyQA~\citep{geva2021did}. 
% Both datasets feature parallel queries and documents in Korean and English.
%, enabling a comprehensive evaluation of both CLIR and Mono-Lingual IR performance
We report NDCG@10~\citep{wang2013theoretical} as our primary evaluation metric.
\paragraph{Data Notation}
We use specific notations to describe the language composition of our training data and evaluation tasks. The training data language notation $\mathcal{D}(l_1l_2l_3)$ specifies the languages of the query, positive, and negatives sequentially. For example, $\mathcal{D}(koenko)$ indicates a dataset with an Korean query, an English positive, and Korean negatives. Task notations $\mathcal{T}(l_1\texttt{-}l_2)$ indicate the languages of the query and the document pool, respectively. For instance, $\mathcal{T}(en\texttt{-}ko)$ refers to a task for retrieving relevant documents from a Korean document pool given an English query.

\section{Experiments and Analysis}
\subsection{Cross-Lingual Retrieval Results}
On the left side of Table~\ref{tab:main1}, we present a comprehensive summary of CLIR performance for the base models and all eight training data language combinations.
% The data language notation $\mathcal{D}(l_1l_2l_3)$ specifies the languages of the query, positive, and negatives sequentially (e.g., $\mathcal{D}(koenko)$ indicates a dataset with a Korean query, English positive, and Korean negatives). Task notations $\mathcal{T}(l_1\texttt{-}l_2)$ indicate the languages of the query and the document pool, respectively (e.g., $\mathcal{T}(en\texttt{-}ko)$ refers to retrieving relevant documents from a Korean document pool given an English query).

\paragraph{Enhancement by Cross-Lingual Query-Positive}
Training with query-positive pairs that mirror the target language direction of a given CLIR task consistently improves performance. For example, in the $\mathcal{T}(en\texttt{-}ko)$ task, the bge-m3 model trained with $\mathcal{D}(enkoen)$ achieves a higher AVG score (86.64) than when trained with $\mathcal{D}(enenko)$ (84.93). Similarly, for the mE5-base model on the $\mathcal{T}(ko\texttt{-}en)$ task, training with $\mathcal{D}(koenen)$ (73.26) outperforms training with $\mathcal{D}(kokoen)$ (68.62).
These results suggest that cross-lingual query-positive pairs encourage the model to learn semantic similarities across linguistic barriers, thereby enhancing CLIR performance. This approach can help mitigate language-specific biases and enable the model to develop a more balanced multilingual representation~\citep{lawrie2023neural, yang2024language}.

\paragraph{Improvement by Mono-Lingual Data}
Interestingly, for some models, training with exclusively mono-lingual data (where the languages of the query ($Q_L$), positive ($P_L$), and negatives ($N_L$) are all the same, i.e., $Q_{L} = P_{L} = N_{L}$) also leads to slight improvements in CLIR performance on both tasks. This observation aligns with findings from the mSimCSE study~\citep{wang2022english}, which indicate that fine-tuning with contrastive loss on mono-lingual datasets can still foster a degree of cross-lingual alignment.

\paragraph{Enhancement by Positive-Negatives Match}
Finally, our results demonstrate that the highest CLIR performance is achieved when the query and positive languages differ, but the positive and negatives share the same language ($Q_{L}\neq P_{L} \;\&\; P_{L} = N_{L}$). For example, for mE5-large on the OVR score, the model trained with $\mathcal{D}(koenen)$ significantly outperforms the model trained with $\mathcal{D}(koenko)$ (84.56 vs. 78.00). Similarly, for mgte-base, the model trained with $\mathcal{D}(enkoko)$ achieves higher OVR performance (80.81) compared to the model trained with $\mathcal{D}(enkoen)$ (78.31). These findings suggest that if $Q_{L}\neq P_{L} \;\&\; P_{L}=N_{L}$, the model learns to distinguish fine-grained semantics in the target language, thereby boosting CLIR capability. Conversely, if $Q_{L}\neq P_{L} \;\&\; Q_{L}=N_{L}$ (e.g., $\mathcal{D}(koenko)$, $\mathcal{D}(enkoen)$), 
