the model may prefer any language differing from the query language. This can enhance sensitivity to language differences but reduce discernment of subtle target language semantics, thus lowering CLIR performance.


\subsection{Mono-Lingual Retrieval Results}
The right side of Table~\ref{tab:main1} details the Mono-Lingual IR performance for models trained on all data combinations. In stark contrast to the CLIR results, Mono-Lingual IR performance generally tends to decrease after training.

% \paragraph{Impact of Mono-Lingual Training Data}
We first note that bge-m3, mE5-large, and mE5-base already demonstrate strong mono-lingual alignment. These models achieve high AVG scores, exceeding 92.0 on $\mathcal{T}(ko\texttt{-}ko)$ and 95.0 on $\mathcal{T}(en\texttt{-}en)$ for the Belebele dataset. Thus, training with mono-lingual dataset ($\mathcal{D}(kokoko), \mathcal{D}(enenen)$) on these well-aligned models can degrade mono-lingual performance. Conversely, the mgte-base model, which exhibits lower mono-lingual performance, shows clear improvements when trained on such mono-lingual datasets. For instance, in $\mathcal{T}(ko\texttt{-}ko)$, training mgte-base with $\mathcal{D}(kokoko)$ significantly boosts its average score from 81.54 to 84.47.

\paragraph{Degradation by Query-Negatives Match}
% A notable observation is the significant drop in Mono-Lingual IR performance under a specific training condition we term "Query-Negatives Match." This scenario arises when 
% % a query and its positive example are in different languages ($Q_L \neq P_L$), while the query and its negative examples are in the same language ($Q_L = N_L$). 
% $Q_{L}\neq P_{L} \;\&\; Q_{L} = N_{L}$
A notable observation is the performance drop in Mono-Lingual IR under a training condition we term "Query-Negatives Match," defined as $Q_{L}\neq P_{L} \;\&\; Q_{L} = N_{L}$.
For instance, considering the OVR score for mE5-base, training on $\mathcal{D}(koenko)$ (80.56) and $\mathcal{D}(enkoen)$ (84.59) exhibits substantial performance degradation compared to the base model (87.06). In contrast, models trained on $\mathcal{D}(koenen)$ or $\mathcal{D}(enkoko)$ (where $Q_{L}\neq P_{L} \;\&\; P_{L} = N_{L}$) experience only minor decreases, or in the case of mgte-base, even show improvements.
In "Query-Negatives Match" scenarios, the model's attempt to link the query with a positive(different from the query language), while simultaneously separating from in-batch negatives (where the dominant linguistic component consists of the query language) directly impairs Mono-Lingual IR performance.

