\section{Related Works}
Information Retrieval (IR) is a critical task that involves effectively retrieving relevant documents given a query~\citep{singhal2001modern, zhu2023large, li2025matching}. Various approaches have been proposed for this task, including dense retrieval~\citep{karpukhin2020dense, xiong2020approximate, wang2024multilingual, zhang2024mgte, chen-etal-2024-m3}, sparse retrieval~\citep{dai2020context, formal2021splade, formal2021spladev2}, and multi-vector retrieval~\citep{khattab2020colbert, liu2023understanding}. The majority of these methods focus primarily on monolingual scenarios, where the query and the document pool are in the same language.

Recently, as access to multilingual documents has become easier, research on Cross-Lingual Information Retrieval (CLIR)—retrieving relevant documents from a pool in a language different from the query—has gained attention~\citep{goswami2021cross, yu2021cross, litschko2022cross, huang2023improving, nguyen2025improving}.

However, previous work on CLIR has rarely addressed data-centric studies. Therefore, in this study, we analyze the impact of various training data language pair combinations on CLIR performance between Korean and English. Our experiments reveal that while certain combinations improve CLIR performance, they often lead to reduced mono-lingual IR performance, highlighting a trade-off. To mitigate this issue, we apply a model merging technique and empirically demonstrate its effectiveness in alleviating the trade-off between the two tasks.

