% Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in scenarios where effective human interaction is crucial, from education and healthcare to creative assistance. The Cattell's 16 Personality Factor (16PF) framework is a fine-grained, psychometrically validated alternative to the OCEAN's Big Five Personality Factors framework, allowing expressive control over 16 distinct traits. In this study, we expand AI personality modeling by transitioning from OCEAN's Big Five model to Cattell's 16 PF model, extending the range of traits represented. Our objectives are fivefold: create an inventory to measure all 16 traits from the 16PF model, enabling comprehensive personality profiling; conduct a cross-model comparison of trait expression across state-of-the-art LLMs, highlighting behavioral divergences linked to training datasets and methods; improve upon existing prompting techniques to enable targeted induction of each 16PF trait; develop a framework for measuring the intensity of trait expression; and design a mechanism for inducing precise intensity levels across traits, allowing fine-grained personality control. We validate our method by applying it to three advanced LLMs- \texttt{GPT-4o}, \texttt{Claude 3.7 Sonnet}, and \texttt{Gemini 2.5 Flash}-and analyze their responses under controlled intensity modulations. This work lays the foundation for building adaptive, controllable AI personas that flexibly adjust their behavioral profiles to match diverse interactional needs and contexts.
%This work introduces a structured, intensity-based approach to personality induction in large language models (LLMs), expanding the previous state-of-the-art methodology, which was limited to binary trait presence using the Big Five framework. Cattell's 16 Personality Factor (16PF) model extends the range of traits represented, extending the scope of AI personality modeling. We extend the Machine Personality Inventory (MPI) test to measure all 16 traits from the 16PF model, providing a comprehensive personality assessment; modify the P2 Prompting method to support targeted induction of each 16PF trait; develop a technique to quantify the intensity of each trait's expression; and design an approach for inducing specific intensity levels across traits, allowing for precise personality manipulation. We validate our method by applying it to three advanced LLMs and analyze their responses under controlled changes in trait intensity modulations. Our approach opens new pathways for controlled, nuanced human-machine interactions in domains such as healthcare, education, and interviewing processes, bringing us one step closer to truly human-like social machines. This work lays the foundation for building adaptive, controllable AI personas that can flexibly adjust their behavioral profiles to match diverse interactional needs and contexts.
Large language models (LLMs) have gained significant traction across a wide range of fields in recent years. There is also a growing expectation for them to display human-like personalities during interactions. To meet this expectation, numerous studies have proposed methods for modelling LLM personalities through psychometric evaluations.
However, most existing models face two major limitations: they rely on the Big Five (OCEAN) framework, which only provides coarse personality dimensions, and they lack mechanisms for controlling trait intensity. In this paper, we address this gap by extending the Machine Personality Inventory (MPI), which originally used the Big Five model, to incorporate the 16 Personality Factor (16PF) model, allowing expressive control over sixteen distinct traits. We also developed a structured framework known as Specific Attribute Control (SAC) for evaluating and dynamically inducing trait intensity in LLMs.
Our method introduces adjective-based semantic anchoring to guide trait intensity expression and leverages behavioural questions across five intensity factors: \textit{Frequency}, \textit{Depth}, \textit{Threshold}, \textit{Effort}, and \textit{Willingness}. 
Applying this methodology to three advanced LLMs - \texttt{GPT-4o}, \texttt{Claude 3.7 Sonnet}, and \texttt{Gemini 2.5 Flash} - we probe into the reliability of our method and test its generalizability.
Through experimentation, we find that modelling intensity as a continuous spectrum yields substantially more consistent and controllable personality expression compared to binary trait toggling.
Moreover, we observe that changes in target trait intensity systematically influence closely related traits in psychologically coherent directions, suggesting that LLMs internalize multi-dimensional personality structures rather than treating traits in isolation. Our work opens new pathways for controlled and nuanced human-machine interactions in domains such as healthcare, education, and interviewing processes, bringing us one step closer to truly human-like social machines.

