    \vspace{0.5cm}
    \label{fig:mpi_prompt}
\end{figure}

Early studies on LLMs hinted that models could mimic certain personality-like attributes ~\cite{Binz_2023,dasgupta2024languagemodelshumanlikecontent,park2023generativeagentsinteractivesimulacra,webb2023emergentanalogicalreasoninglarge,ziems2024largelanguagemodelstransform}, though these were typically small-scale demonstrations in ad hoc settings rather than standardized evaluations.
A major advancement came with the Machine Personality Inventory (MPI) and P2 prompting~\cite{jiang2022evaluating}, which jointly provided a psychometrically-informed framework for evaluating LLMs’ personality consistency and inducing specific traits without fine-tuning. Subsequent work~\cite{SerapioGarcia2023Personality} further validated the reliability and construct validity of inferred personality scores in LLMs.

In our work, we adopt the 16PF model, while introducing a structured intensity modulation framework, and analyzing latent trait interdependencies, which were not supported in earlier work.

