\section{CMF Ratios, and CMF Limits}\label{sec:CMF_Ratios}
This section will define CMF limits, and CMF ratios, which are generalizations of Ap\'ery limits and D-finite ratios.  The remainder of this document will discuss, both phenomenologically and rigorously, the properties and relations of CMF ratios.
\begin{definition}
Let $\clM$ be a CMF of dimension $d$ rank $r$ over $\Q$. Select a trajectory $x+nv$ such that $v\in\Z^d,x\in\Q^d$, and four vectors $p,p',q,q'\in \Q^r$.\\
Then, the \textit{CMF Ratio} is defined as the following sequence:
    \begin{equation} \label{eq:CMF_limit_def}
        \clL_{x,v}^{p,p',q,q'}(n) \coloneqq \frac{p^t\cdot \clM_{nv}(x)\cdot p'}{q^t\cdot \clM_{nv}(x)\cdot q'}.
    \end{equation}
For brevity, denote $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}\coloneqq\clL_{x,v}^{p,e_r,q,e_r}$. The ratio $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}$ can also be thought of as a ratio of the inner products of $p$ and $q$ with the last column of $\clM_{nv}(x)$. Finally, the limit of $\clL_{x,v}^{p,p',q,q'}(n)$ is called a \textit{CMF Limit}.
\end{definition}
\begin{examp}
Consider the CMF generated by $\pFq{2}{1}{x_1,x_2}{x_3}{-1}$. This CMF can be obtained from the CMF detailed in Examples \ref{ex:2f1CMF_first_example},\ref{ex:2f1_generation}, if one substitutes $z=-1$. Let us calculate and graph a few of its CMF ratios:\\
    First: $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$ for $x = v = (1,1,2),p = (0,1),q = (-2,2)$. Let us first calculate $\clM_{nv}(x)$ for a few values of $n$:
\[\clM_0(x) = I, \clM_v(x) = \begin{pmatrix}
    -6&18\\-24&66
\end{pmatrix},\clM_{2v}(x) = \begin{pmatrix}
    -150&660\\-540&2370
\end{pmatrix}, \clM_{3v}(x) =\begin{pmatrix}
    - \frac{10220}{3} & \frac{62300}{3}\\- \frac{36680}{3} & \frac{223580}{3}
\end{pmatrix}. \]
Hence:
$$\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(0) = \frac{1}{2},\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(1) = \frac{11}{16},\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(2) = \frac{79}{114},\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(3) = \frac{1597}{2304}.$$
Or in decimal form:
$$\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n) = (0.5,0.6875,0.6930\dots,0.69314\dots,\dots)$$
The sequence appears to converge to $\log(2)\approx0.693147\dots$. Moreover, it has a non-trivial convergence rate $\rho = -3.53\dots$ and seems to have a non trivial and positive (!) irrationality measure $\delta \approx 0.31$.\\
In contrast, consider the CMF ratio $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$ for $x = v = (-1,-1,2),p = (0,1),q = (-2,2)$. 
The first few values are:
$$\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n) = (\frac{1}{2},\frac{19}{60}, \frac{1109}{5460},\frac{713}{13860},- \frac{327713}{540540},\dots)$$
This sequence does not seem to converge. The first 300 values of this sequence are plotted in Figure \ref{fig:2f1NonConverging}
\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{Figures/Scatter2f1.png}
    \caption{Example of non-converging CMF ratio - First 300 values of $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$ }
    \label{fig:2f1NonConverging}
\end{figure}
\end{examp}
It is useful to consider $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$  where $T_{x,v}(n)$ is in companion form, and later, to compare CMF ratios of coboundary equivalent CMFs.
\begin{prop}\label{prop:limit_of_companion_is_apery_weak}
If $T_{x,v}(n)$ is in companion form and the CMF Ratio $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$ converges, then it is a D-finite ratio (implying the CMF limit is an Ap\'ery limit). 
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    Given \eqref{eq:prod_of_traj}, for a trajectory matrix in companion form, the last column of $\clM_{nv}(x)$ contains the solutions to the recursion contained in $T_{x,v}(n)$, with initial conditions $e_1,\dots,e_r$. Thus, $p^t\cdot \clM_{nv}(x)\cdot e_r$ is the solution with initial condition $p\in \Q^r$, making $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}$ a D-finite ratio.
\end{proof}
\begin{corollary}
    Any D-finite ratio is a CMF ratio (i.e., any Ap\'ery limit is a CMF limit)
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Let $u_p(n)$ and $ u_q(n)$ be solutions to a minimal recursion $L\in \Q(n)[S_n]$ of order $r$, with initial conditions $p$ and $q$ respectively. Then, we can calculate the companion matrix $M(n)\in\text{GL}_r(\Q(n))$ encoding $L$. The matrix $M(n)$ generates a CMF of dimension $1$ and rank $r$ (see Example  \ref{ex:1dCMF}), such that the trajectory matrix $T_{0,1}(n) = M(n)$. Thus, the generated CMF has a CMF ratio $\clL^{p,q}_{0,1}(n)$ which is equal to the desired D-finite ratio.
\end{proof}
\begin{prop}\label{prop:limit_of_companion_is_apery_strong}
 Suppose for a CMF $\clM$, the trajectory matrix $T_{x,v}(n)$ is in companion form. Suppose further, that the recurrence operator $L$ encoded by $T_{x,v}(n)$ satisfies the conditions of Poincar\'e and Perron, and is irreducible (in the sense of definition 2 in \cite{Wimp1983}).\\
 Then, for any $a(n)\in\Q(n)^r$, and any $p,q$ such that $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$ converges, $\lim_{n\to\infty} \clL_{x,v}^{p,a(n),q,a(n)}(n) = \lim_{n\to\infty} \clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$. Furthermore, both have the same convergence rate.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
This case is similar to the proof above of Proposition \ref{prop:limit_of_companion_is_apery_weak}, but instead of a simple ratio of solutions $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n) = \frac{u_p(n)}{u_q(n)}$, we have $\clL_{x,v}^{p,a(n),q,a(n)}(n) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i(n)u_p(n-(i-1))}{\sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i(n)u_q(n-(i-1))}$. From Poincar\'e--Perron, we have:
    $$\sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i(n)u_p(n-(i-1)) = \sum_{j=1}^r c_j \sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i(n)\lambda_j^{n-(i-1)}f_j(n-(i-1)).$$
Notice that the irreducibility of $L$ implies $\sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i(n)\lambda_j^{n-(i-1)}f_j(n-(i-1))\neq0$, as that would imply the solution $\lambda_j^nf_j(n)$ has a smaller annihilator. This implies that if we define:
    $$g_j(n) \coloneqq \sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i(n)\lambda_j^{-(i-1)}f_j(n-(i-1))$$
    then $\frac{f_j(n+1)}{f_j(n)}\to1\implies\frac{g_j(n+1)}{g_j(n)}\to1$. This along with:
    $$\sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i(n)u_p(n-(i-1)) = \sum_{i=1}^{r} c_i\lambda_i^ng_i(n)$$
    gives exactly the same setup for the proof of Proposition \ref{prop:apery_limit_convergence}. Thus giving that:
    $$\lim_{n\to\infty} \clL_{x,v}^{p,a(n),q,a(n)}(n) = \frac{c_j}{\Tilde{c_j}} = \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{u_p(n)}{u_q(n)}=\lim_{n\to\infty} \clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$$
    $$\rho = \log\abs{\frac{\lambda_k}{\lambda_j}}$$
    with $j,k$ defined as in Proposition \ref{prop:apery_limit_convergence}
\end{proof}


In most cases, trajectory matrices are not in companion form. Thus, let us examine the effect of coboundary equivalence on CMF ratios and limits:
\begin{prop}\label{prop:limits_of_companions_weak}
For two coboundary CMFs $\clM\sim \overline{\clM}\ , \ \clM_v =A\overline{\clM_v}\sigma_v(A^{-1})$, with resulting ratios $\clL,\overline{\clL}$ respectively, then:    
$$\clL_{x,v}^{p,p',q,q'}(n) =\overline{\clL}_{x,v}^{A^t(x)p,A^{-1}(x+nv)p',A^t(x)q,A^{-1}(x+nv)q'}(n) $$
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
    $$\clL_{x,v}^{p,p',q,q'}(n) =  \frac{p^t\cdot \clM_{nv}(x)\cdot p'}{q^t\cdot \clM_{nv}(x)\cdot q'} =  \frac{p^t\cdot [A\overline{\clM}_{nv}\sigma_{nv}(A^{-1})](x)\cdot p'}{q^t\cdot [A\overline{\clM}_{nv}\sigma_{nv}(A^{-1})](x)\cdot q'} =$$ $$=\frac{p^t\cdot A(x)\overline{\clM}_{nv}(x)A^{-1}(x+nv)\cdot p'}{q^t\cdot A(x)\overline{\clM}_{nv}(x)A^{-1}(x+nv)\cdot q'} =\overline{\clL}_{x,v}^{A^t(x)p,A^{-1}(x+nv)p',A^t(x)q,A^{-1}(x+nv)q'}(n) $$

\end{proof}
\begin{corollary}\label{Cor:limits_of_companions_strong}
for a CMF $\clM$, and its ratio $\clL$, if, for certain $x,v$, there exists $A\in\text{GL}_r(\Q(\textbf{x}))$ s.t $C(n)\coloneqq A(x+(n-1)v)T_{x,v}(n)A^{-1}(x+nv)$ is in companion form, and encodes an irreducible Poincar\'e-Perron recurrence $L$, then $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$ has the same limit, and the same convergence rate, as the D-finite ratio of $L$, with initial values $A^t(x)p,A^t(x)q$
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
    The coboundary transform $A$ connecting $C(n)$ and $T_{x,v}(n)$ implies that for $\clM\sim \overline{\clM}\ , \ \clM_v =A\overline{\clM_v}\sigma_v(A^{-1})$, we have that the trajectory matrix $\overline{T}_{x,v}(n) = C(n)$, as required in Proposition \ref{prop:limit_of_companion_is_apery_strong}.
Thus, from Proposition \ref{prop:limits_of_companions_weak}
$$\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n) =\overline{\clL}_{x,v}^{A^t(x)p,A^{-1}(x+nv)e_r,A^t(x)q,A^{-1}(x+nv)e_r}(n) $$ Which has the same limit and convergence rate as $ \frac{u_{A^t(x)p}(n)}{u_{A^t(x)q}(n)}$
\end{proof}
\begin{examp} \label{ex:Zeta3RecreateApery}
Recall Example \ref{ex:Zeta3TrajCompanion}. There, a coboundary matrix is provided, that connects the companion matrix of Ap\'ery's recurrence $L$, with the trajectory matrix $T_{(1,1),(1,1)}(n)$ and satisfies Corollary \ref{Cor:limits_of_companions_strong}. Thus, we know that $\clL_{(1,1),(1,1)}^{(0,1),(1,1)}(n)$ converges to and has the same convergence rate as the D-finite ratio of $L$, with initial values $A^t(x)p,A^t(x)q$. As $A^t(1,1) = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\-1 & 6\end{pmatrix}$ we have the initial conditions: 
$$u_1(0) = 0, u_1(1) = 6, u_2(0) = 1, u_2(1) = 5$$
Exactly as in Theorem \ref{thm:aperys_theorem}. Thus $\clL_{(1,1),(1,1)}^{(0,1),(1,1)}(n)$ converges to $\zeta(3)$, with convergence rate $\rho = -8\log(\sqrt{2}+1)$. In fact, it can also be shown it is similarly bounded in height. This exemplifies an empirical observation:  the irrationality measure $\delta$ for the CMF ratio in Corollary \ref{Cor:limits_of_companions_strong} is identical to that of the corresponding D-finite ratio. This phenomena is used extensively in \cite{RazUnifying2025}.
\end{examp}
Summarizing, the above demonstrates that CMF ratios and limits, a higher dimensional generalization of D-finite ratios and Ap\'ery limits, inherit their useful properties in Proposition \ref{prop:apery_limit_convergence}, at least under some reasonable assumptions. The following section will demonstrate some phenomenological properties of specific CMFs and CMF limits, formulate them into conjectures, prove some special cases, and suggest some applications and corollaries.
