\section{Experimental Analysis of Conservative Matrix Fields}\label{sec:Experiments}
The previous section demonstrates how to construct a sequence $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(n)$, with similar properties to D-finite ratios. One can analyze the behavior of the limit and the convergence of these sequences when changing the parameters $x,v,p,q$. This section will focus on variations in the direction $v$. First, one can note how they are effected by scaling:  $v\to kv$ for $k\in\N\setminus\set{0}$:
\begin{remark}\label{rem:homogeneous_CMF_quants}
For a CMF ratio $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(n)$ that converges to $l$ with non-trivial convergence rate $\rho<0$, the CMF ratio $\clL^{p,q}_{x,kv}(n),k\in\N\setminus\set{0}$ has the same limit $l$, and a convergence rate of $k\rho$. If furthermore $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(n)$ has a non-trivial irrationality measure $\delta>-1$, then $\clL^{p,q}_{x,kv}(n)$ has the same irrationality measure.\\
Suppose additionally  $T_{x,v}(n)$ is coboundary equivalent to an irreducible Poincar\'e-Perron recurrence $L$, with eigenvalues $(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_r)$. then $T_{kx,v}(n)$ is coboundary equivalent to a Poincar\'e-Perron recurrence $L'$, with eigenvalues $(\lambda_1^k,\dots,\lambda_r^k)$.
\end{remark}
\begin{proof}
As $\clL^{p,q}_{x,kv}(n)$ is a sub-sequence of $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(n)$, the first two points are an immediate corollary of Remark \ref{rem:convergence_height_delta_connection}. The final point is clear as the conditions on $L$ imply $\exists \sum_{i=0}^r a_i(n)S_n^{ki}\in\langle L\rangle$. Defining $L' \coloneqq \sum_{i=0}^r a_i(n)S_n^{i}$, implies for any solution $u(n)$ of $L$, that $u(kn)$ is a solution of $L'$. This demonstrates that $L'$ has the desired eigenvalues. One can construct a basis changing matrix from $(u(n),u(n+1),\dots,u(n+r-1))$ to $(u(n),u(n+k),\dots,u(n+k(r-1)))$, which will provide a coboundary transform between $T_{x,kv}(n)$ and the companion matrix of $L'$. 
\end{proof}
Hence, under some assumptions of existence, the limit, $\delta$, $\rho$, $\eta$ and $\log\abs{\lambda_i}$ of $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(n)$ are homogeneous as functions of $v$. The limit and irrationality measures are 0-homogeneous, and the convergence rate, height and $\log\abs{\lambda_i}$ are 1-homogeneous. This implies these quantities depend mostly on direction in $\Z^d$. This observation suggests the analysis of the quantities $l,\delta,\frac{\rho}{\abs{v}},\frac{\log\abs{\lambda_i}}{\abs{v}}$ as functions of the direction of $v$.
We will select specific CMFs and parameters $x,p,q$, and graph empirical evaluations of these quantities. We will sample $v$ from a set of lattice points in $\Z^d\cap B(0,R)$, for which $\forall_{n\in\N}\ \clM_{nv}(x)\in\text{GL}_r(\Q)$. While $\frac{\log\abs{\lambda_i}}{\abs{v}}$ can be calculated in closed form, the other parameters must be approximated. This will be done by evaluating late elements in the sequence,  $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(n)$. for $N \gg 1$: $$\hat{l} = \clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(N)\approx\lim_{n\to\infty}\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(n)=l\quad \hat{\rho} =\frac{\log\abs{\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(N)-\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(2N)}}{N} \approx\rho$$
$$\hat{\delta} = -1-\frac{\log\abs{\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(N)-\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(2N)}}{\log\abs{H(\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(N))}}\approx \delta $$
These approximations are appropriate as $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(N) \approx l\pm e^{N\rho}$, implying:

\[\frac{\log\abs{\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(N)-\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(2N)}}{N}\approx\frac{1}{N} \log\abs{e^{N\rho}\pm e^{2N\rho}} =\]
\[=\log\abs{e^\rho}+\frac{1}{N}\log\abs{1\pm e^{N\rho}}\approx \rho\pm \frac{e^{N\rho}}{N}\approx \rho\]
and the rest follow.\par
First, we will examine the CMF detailed in Example \ref{ex:Zeta3CMF}. It is generated by:
