\subsection{Observations and Conjectures}
This subsection details some observations arising from the simulations graphed in Figures \ref{fig:zeta3panels}-\ref{fig:C3x3panels}. Based on these observations, we propose several conjectures to motivate future research.
\par
Upon examining Figures \ref{fig:zeta3panels}-\ref{fig:C3x3panels}, one observes that where it is defined, the CMF limit $\lim_{n\to\infty}\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$ appears to be constant as function of the direction of $v$, except for finite number of discontinuities. Moreover, in domains where the convergence rate is not trivial, the limit is defined and seems constant. This observation is formalized in the following conjecture:
\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:cont_limit}
Let $v_0\in\Z^d\setminus\set{0}$, and suppose $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v_0}(n)$ converges to $l\in\R$, with non-trivial convergence rate. Then there exists an angular domain $D_{v_0}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ around $\frac{v_0}{\abs{v_0}}$ such that for all $v$ in that angular domain (meaning $v\in\set{v\in\Z^d\setminus\set{0}:\frac{v}{\abs{v}}\in D_{v_0}}$) , $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}(n)\to l$.
\end{conjecture}
Upon examining Figures \ref{fig:zeta3panels}-\ref{fig:C3x3panels}, one observes that $\delta,\frac{\rho}{\abs{v}},\frac{\log\abs{\lambda_i}}{\abs{v}}$ are all continuous with respect to the direction of $v$ (at least where these are defined). These observations are formalized in the following conjectures:
\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:cont_delta}
Let $v_0\in\Z^d\setminus\set{0}$, and suppose $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v_0}(n)$ converges to $l\in\R$, with non-trivial convergence rate $\rho<0$ and irrationality measure $\delta>-1$. Then, for any $\varepsilon>0$, there exists an angular domain $D_{v_0}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ around $\frac{v_0}{\abs{v_0}}$ such that for all $v\in D_{v_0}$, the irrationality measure of $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}$ is in $(\delta-\varepsilon,\delta+\varepsilon)$
\end{conjecture}
Either verifying, or disproving Conjecture \ref{conj:cont_delta} seems challenging, due to the difficulty of establishing the irrationality measure of any particular sequence.
\begin{conjecture}
    \label{conj:cont_rho}
Let $v_0\in\Z^d\setminus\set{0}$, and suppose $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v_0}(n)$ converges to $l\in\R$, with non-trivial convergence rate $\rho<0$. Then, for any $\varepsilon>0$, there exists an angular domain $D_{v_0}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ around $\frac{v_0}{\abs{v_0}}$ such that for all $v\in D_{v_0}$, the normalized convergence rate of $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v}$ is in $(\frac{\rho}{\abs{v_0}}-\varepsilon,\frac{\rho}{\abs{v_0}}+\varepsilon)$
\end{conjecture}
\begin{conjecture} \label{conj:cont_eigs}
Let $v_0\in\Z^d\setminus\set{0}$, and suppose  $T_{x,v_0}(n)$ is coboundary equivalent to a companion matrix whose recurrence $L$ satisfies the Poincar\'e Perron conditions, with eigenvalues $(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_r)$. Then, for any $\varepsilon>0$, there exists an angular domain $D_{v_0}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ around $\frac{v_0}{\abs{v_0}}$ such that for all $v\in D_{v_0}$, $T_{x,v}(n)$ is coboundary equivalent to a companion matrix who satisfies the Poincar\'e Perron conditions, and the $i$-th normalized eigenvalue of $T_{x,v}(n)$ is in $(\frac{\log\abs{\lambda_i}}{\abs{v_0}}-\varepsilon,\frac{\log\abs{\lambda_i}}{\abs{v_0}}+\varepsilon)$
\end{conjecture}
\begin{remark}
    Notice that as detailed in Remark \ref{rem:convergence_height_delta_connection}, the height of a sequence can be directly ascertained from its convergence rate and irrationality measure (if both are non trivial). Specifically:
    $$\eta = -\frac{\rho}{\delta+1}$$
    Thus, if Conjectures \ref{conj:cont_delta},\ref{conj:cont_rho} hold, we have that the \textit{normalized height} $\frac{\eta}{\abs{v}}$ is also continuous. Furthermore, if any two of $\delta, \frac{\rho}{\abs{v}},\frac{\eta}{\abs{v}}$ are continuous in the direction of $v$, then they all are. 
\end{remark}
It is straight forward to prove Conjectures \ref{conj:cont_limit},\ref{conj:cont_rho} and \ref{conj:cont_eigs} for the constant CMF described in Example \ref{ex:Const3x3}. This, by noting that as $M_{x_1},M_{x_2}$ commute, they share eigen spaces. And as they are both diagonalizeable, they are simultaneously diagonalizeable. As similarity over $\text{GL}_r(\C)$ is a special case of coboundary equivalence, our CMF is coboundary equivalent to one generated by:
$$\overline{M}_{x_1} = \begin{pmatrix}
    5&0&0\\
    0&3&0\\
    0&0&1
\end{pmatrix},\overline{M}_{x_2} = \begin{pmatrix}
    \frac{5}{3}&0&0\\
    0&\frac{1}{3}&0\\
    0&0&4
\end{pmatrix}$$
and using Proposition \ref{prop:limits_of_companions_weak}, the rest falls out naturally. The differences in $\rho,\delta$ between the two ratios $\clL_{x,v}^{p_1,q_1}(n),\clL_{x,v}^{p_2,q_2}(n)$ can be explained by $p_2,q_2$ being spanned by only two of the eigen vectors of $M_{x_1},M_{x_2}$. It is not hard to generalize these arguments and justify Conjectures \ref{conj:cont_limit},\ref{conj:cont_rho}, and \ref{conj:cont_eigs} for any conservative matrix field with constant matrices.
\subsection{Applications and Corollaries}
Conjectures \ref{conj:cont_limit},\ref{conj:cont_delta}, if true, imply interesting properties, and motivate algorithms for the search and analysis of good approximations for constants.
\begin{corollary}
Assuming Conjectures  \ref{conj:cont_limit},\ref{conj:cont_delta} hold for a CMF $\clM$ of dimension $d$ and rank $r$, and suppose further $\clL_{x,v_0}^{p,q}(n)$ converges to $l\in\R$ with irrationality measure $\delta>0$. Then there exists an angular domain $D_{v_0}\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ around $\frac{v_0}{\abs{v_0}}$ such that for all $v$ in that angular domain (meaning $v\in\set{v\in\Z^d\setminus\set{0}:\frac{v}{\abs{v}}\in D_{v_0}}$) , $\clL^{p,q}_{x,v_0}(n)\to l$ with positive irrationality measure.\\
Put simply - one irrationality proving sequence in a CMF, implies infinitely many (essentially different) irrationality proving sequences.
\end{corollary}
\begin{remark}\label{rem:Gradient_Ascent}
Assuming Conjectures  \ref{conj:cont_limit},\ref{conj:cont_delta} hold for a CMF $\clM$ of dimension $d$ and rank $r$, and suppose that $\clL_{x,v_0}^{p,q}(n)$ converges to $l\in\R$ with irrationality measure $\delta>-1$. Then, it is possible to use an optimization algorithm to search for $v$ such that $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$ converges to $l$ with higher irrationality measure.
\end{remark}
Let us expand on Remark \ref{rem:Gradient_Ascent}. Often, when D-finite ratios appear in the literature, they are generated as a sequence of values from some D-finite function, usually of several variables. like hypergeometric functions, integrals in several variables, binomial sums, etc. Thus, one can examine record results for $\zeta(5),\pi,G,\dots$, generate a CMF from the multivariate D-finite function used in their construction, and associate the D-finite ratio with a CMF ratio $\clL_{x,v}^{p,q}(n)$. Then, the usage of optimization algorithms as described in Remark \ref{rem:Gradient_Ascent} might result in an improvement to the record result.\\
This, we hope, should not only generate interest in CMF ratios, but also in proving the suggested conjectures.
