\paragraph{Information Content of Query Points.}
Next, we investigate the information content of the query points. We are mainly interested in whether query points represent local or global information. Local information means that each query point represents the solution of the nearby surroundings while global information means that a single query point influences the global spatial domain. Due to the constrained receptive field, we assume that each token only represents local information. We add Gaussian noise to a token and investigate how the solution is affected to validate the hypothesis. \Cref{fig:noisy-latents} shows that only the surrounding of the token is affected which matches our hypothesis that each token represents local information.

