\subsubsection{Evaluation on Real data}
We also evaluate the proposed model on a real-life multi-speaker dataset CH109. The baseline is a cascade streaming multi-talker ASR model, which consists of a streaming single speaker ASR model and a streaming diarization model. In the cascaded model, words and speaker segments are matched using time-stamps. The ASR model for the cascaded baseline is the same as the model whose parameters are employed for initialization for streaming model training, as mentioned in Section \ref{sec:train}, and the streaming diarization model for the baseline is the same as the model used for streaming SSA inference, as mentioned in Section \ref{sec:finetune}.~The proposed SSA model demonstrates superior performance on the CH109 dataset, achieving a cpWER of 26.21\% at a latency of 1120 ms. This outperforms not only the cascaded streaming model but also the performance of the offline system \cite{park2024enhancing}, despite our proposed system operating under low-latency streaming constraints. 
% The SSA model’s success is attributed to its end-to-end optimization, which eliminates the error propagation inherent in cascaded systems, and its use of target speaker's activities, which allows it to focus on the target speaker without requiring explicit speaker profiles or enrollment audio. Note that the ASR model in cascaded system is also trained with Fisher data.

% The SSA model’s ability to handle real-world overlapping speech, as evidenced by its strong performance on the CH109 dataset, highlights its robustness and applicability to challenging scenarios such as meeting and conversational talk. Unlike the cascaded model, which combines separate diarization and ASR components, the SSA model integrates speaker adaptation directly into the ASR framework, resulting in improved accuracy and efficiency. Furthermore, its performance is comparable to offline systems, making the SSA model a practical alternative for real-time applications where low latency is critical.

