\subsection{State-of-the-art and Related Works}\label{subsec:related_works}

% This led to the development of the \gls*{danse} algorithm~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010,bertrand_distributed_2010-1} and an improved version thereof including a \gls*{gevd}, the \glsunset{gevddanse}\mbox{\gls*{gevddanse}} algorithm~\cite{hassani_gevd-based_2016}. 
% Both were
Research works in the field of distributed algorithms have so far largely focused on parameter estimation, i.e., estimating a single, static or slowly varying parameter vector (e.g., the dimensions of a room, the location of sound sources, or features of a speech signal) of interest to all nodes in the \gls*{wasn}~\cite{jin-jun_xiao_distributed_2006,schizas_distributed_2007,sayed_diffusion_2013}. 
% More recent efforts have also attempted distributed estimation of node-specific parameter vectors~\cite{plata-chaves_distributed_2015, bahari_distributed_2017, cobos_survey_2017, zhao_model-based_2020}. 
However, in many applications, nodes need to estimate entire signals, i.e., samples of an underlying incoming nonstationary process such as a speech signal~\cite{ying_jia_distributed_2006, doclo_reduced-bandwidth_2009}, which may also be node-specific.
% Estimating each individual sample using a new instance of a parameter estimation method is computationally untractable when considering high sampling rates. 
Distributed node-specific signal estimation can be efficiently tackled by considering the target signals as stochastic processes that emerge from a common set of latent sound sources. In that case, the target signal at any given node can be recovered by filtering all available sensor signals, i.e., performing weighted linear combinations of all sensor signals where the weights are found as solutions to an optimization problem.

To achieve the same performance as a centralized system, a naive solution may be to allow all nodes to broadcast all of their sensor signals. This would, however, result in intolerably high communication bandwidth usage, especially when many nodes are involved. This limitation led to the development of the \gls*{danse} algorithm~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010}, in which nodes first combine their local sensor signals into low-dimensional (i.e., fused) signals which are then broadcast. %In this way, \gls*{danse} addresses the communication efficiency requirement of distributed systems. %The fusion can be also performed as a filtering operation, where the filter weights are regularly updated at each node by solving a \gls*{lmmse} problem and computing a \gls*{mwf} based on the most recently received data.
% This fuse-and-transmit concept is at the core of the \gls*{danse} algorithm~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010}. 
Using the \gls*{danse} algorithm in an \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn}, the same node-specific signal estimation performance as an equivalent centralized system can be obtained in an iterative manner, without relying on a fusion center.
% This is particularly remarkable since the centralized performance cannot be matched when using other data compression techniques such as principal subspace estimation~\cite{li_distributed_2011}.

% \todoi{Concise intro: shorten above paragraphs}

% Recent research  provided algorithmic solutions for, e.g., distributed trace-ratio optimization for dimensionality reduction and signal enhancement~\cite{musluoglu_distributed_2021}, distributed least-mean-square for parameter estimation~\cite{plata-chaves_distributed_2015}, distributed minimum-variance beamforming~\cite{markovich-golan_optimal_2015}, distributed filtering for max signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio~\cite{tavakoli_distributed_2017}, distributed voice activity and speech presence probability estimation~\cite{zhao_model-based_2020, bahari_distributed_2017}, distributed subspace estimation~\cite{li_distributed_2011}, and distributed signal estimation for noise reduction~\cite{bertrand_robust_2009}. In the latter, nodes aim at linearly combining the available sensor signals to estimate a certain target signal, e.g., a desired talker. The coefficients of this linear combination are the values to be estimated. This objective led to the development of the algorithm considered as basis in this work, the \gls*{danse} algorithm~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010,bertrand_distributed_2010-1}, used also in~\cite{bertrand_robust_2009}. Nodes involved in the \gls*{danse} algorithm in an \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn} reach the same performance as an equivalent centralized system while only exchanging low-dimensional fused versions of their local sensor signals. This data fusion mechanism addresses two key challenges of distributed processing as it lowers the communication bandwidth requirements and reduces the computational cost at each node.


The \gls*{danse} algorithm is designed to function solely in \gls*{fc} \glspl*{wasn}, i.e., networks where all nodes can directly transmit data to all other nodes. This motivated the development of alternative formulations, first for \glspl*{wasn} with static tree-topologies~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2011_TDANSE} and static mixed-topologies~\cite{szurley_distributed_2015}, then for any, possibly time-varying topologies via the topology-independent (TI) \gls*{danse} algorithm (\glsunset{tidanse}\gls*{tidanse})~\cite{szurley_topology-independent_2017}.
% with or without \gls*{gevd} (referred to as \glsunset{ti_or_gevddanse}\mbox{\gls*{ti_or_gevddanse}})
This algorithm achieves convergence in any topology-unconstrained \gls*{wasn} by pruning the network to a tree topology at each algorithmic iteration and building the in-network sum of all fused signals, which is then used for node-specific signal estimation.

% The \gls*{tdanse} algorithm~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2011_TDANSE} was first developed for application in \glspl*{wasn} with a fixed tree topology, relaxing the full-connectivity assumption, although lacking the ability to function in the presence of dynamic topologies and link failures. The topology-independent (TI) \gls*{danse} algorithm (\glsunset{tidanse}\mbox{\gls*{tidanse}})~\cite{szurley_topology-independent_2017} was then introduced, broadening the feasibility of distributed node-specific signal estimation to any topology-unconstrained \glspl*{wasn} without topological constraints. This algorithm 
% % with or without \gls*{gevd} (referred to as \glsunset{ti_or_gevddanse}\mbox{\gls*{ti_or_gevddanse}})
% achieves convergence to the centralized solution by, at each iteration, pruning the topology-unconstrained \gls*{wasn} to a new tree topology and building an in-network sum of all fused signals in the network, which is then used for estimating the required parameters at each node. 
% % Recently, a version of \gls*{tidanse} including a \gls*{gevd} (\glsunset{tigevddanse}\mbox{\gls*{tigevddanse}}) was also proposed~\cite{didier2024tigevddanse}.

Even though \gls*{tidanse} enables distributed signal estimation in any \gls*{wasn}, it converges slowly towards the centralized estimate~\cite{szurley_topology-independent_2017,didier2024tigevddanse}. This slow convergence is an inherent characteristic of \gls*{tidanse} since, at each iteration, the updating node only has access to the global in-network sum of all fused signals to update its estimation parameters.
The number of degrees of freedom (\glspl*{dof}) in the local \gls*{tidanse} optimization problem is indeed much smaller than for \gls*{danse} in an equivalent \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn}, where nodes have access to all individual fused signals from all other nodes. Letting $K$ represent the number of nodes in the \gls*{wasn} and $Q$ the number of fused signals computed at and exchanged between each node, an updating node in \gls*{danse} has access to $Q(K-1)$ signals transmitted by other nodes while an updating node in \gls*{tidanse} has access to only $Q$ signals transmitted by other nodes. The resulting issue is particularly noticeable when deploying \gls*{tidanse} in an \gls*{fc}, or nearly \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn}, where convergence to the centralized solution is then significantly slower than that of the \gls*{danse} algorithm. This drawback of \gls*{tidanse} severely hinders its applicability in real-world scenarios that require fast adaptivity. %Furthermore, switching from \gls*{tidanse} to \gls*{danse} when the \gls*{wasn} becomes \gls*{fc} (and back when a link failure occurs) would require a complex coordination protocol at the network-layer, making such strategy highly impractical.

