\subsection{Contributions}


In this paper, a distributed algorithm termed \glsunset{dansep}\gls*{dansep} is proposed to resolve the slow convergence issue of \gls*{tidanse}. The \gls*{dansep} algorithm allows all nodes in any unconstrained and possibly time-varying \gls*{wasn} topology to reach the same node-specific signal estimation performance as if they were part of a centralized system, while only exchanging low-dimensional fused versions of their local sensor signals.
The \gls*{dansep} algorithm achieves this without compromising on convergence speed when the network is \gls*{fc}, i.e., reaching the same convergence speed as \gls*{danse} in \gls*{fc} \glspl*{wasn}. %The proposed algorithm is dubbed \glsunset{dansep}\gls*{dansep}. %, where the ``TI'' prefix has been dropped since this new algorithm can also be applied in \gls*{fc} topologies without sacrificing convergence speed with respect to \gls*{danse}.
% and also uses the concepts of signal fusion, tree-pruning, and in-network summation. 
% Just as in all other versions of \gls*{danse}, the nodes involved in \gls*{dansep} only transmit low-dimensional fused version of their local sensor signals between each other. 


Letting $B\geq 1$ denote the number of direct neighbors of the updating node, the key enabling principle of \gls*{dansep} lies in the fact that the updating node uses $B$ partial in-network sums of fused signals coming from each of its neighbors \textit{separately} to update its estimation parameters. %This is a major difference with respect to \gls*{tidanse}, where the partial sums are themselves summed before being used.

An updating node in \gls*{dansep} thus has access to $QB$ fused signals, against $Q$ for an updating node in \gls*{tidanse}.
% $Q(K{-}1{-}B)$ fewer signals than an updating node in \gls*{danse} in an \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn}.
Since $B=K{-}1$ in an \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn}, \gls*{dansep} converges as fast as \gls*{danse} while saving communication bandwidth since \gls*{dansep} relies on peer-to-peer data transmission, while \gls*{danse} relies on broadcasting from every node to every other node.
The use of partial in-network sums of fused signals coming from different branches in a tree-topology \gls*{wasn} has been explored in~\cite{musluoglu_distributed_2023} for a general signal fusion problem. However, the solution proposed there requires each non-updating node to solve an additional optimization problem, making it significantly more computationally expensive than \gls*{dansep}.

% \todoi{Mention peer-to-peer communication in other places...}

Since the number of \glspl*{dof} in the local optimization problem at an updating node in \gls*{dansep} depends on the number $B$ of direct neighbors of the updating node, the choice of \gls*{wasn} tree-pruning strategy is crucial and impacts the convergence speed of the algorithm.
In this paper, a tree-pruning strategy that maximizes the number of \glspl*{dof} at the updating node is proposed.
Using this strategy, simulated experiments show that \gls*{dansep} converges as fast as \gls*{danse} in \gls*{fc} \glspl*{wasn} and faster than \gls*{tidanse} in any non-\gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn} topology. In general, it is observed that the more connections exist between nodes of the original non-pruned \gls*{wasn}, the faster \gls*{dansep} can converge. %Even when using a different pruning strategy (that may be, e.g., optimal with respect to another criterion), \gls*{dansep} showcases quicker convergence with respect to \gls*{tidanse}. However, in that case, \gls*{dansep} may not converge as fast as \gls*{danse} in \gls*{fc} \glspl*{wasn}.

All in all, the proposed \gls*{dansep} algorithm can be viewed as an all-round alternative to \gls*{danse} and \gls*{tidanse} which (i) merges the advantages of both, (ii) reconciliates their differences into a single formulation, and (iii) shows advantages of its own in terms of communication bandwidth usage in \gls*{fc} \glspl*{wasn}.
