\section{Problem Statement}\label{sec:prob_statement}

Consider a \gls*{wasn} composed of $K$ nodes deployed in an environment with $S\geq 1$ localized sound sources of interest. The set of all node indices is denoted $\K\triangleq \{1,\dots,K\}$. Node $k\in\K$ has $M_k$ local sensors (microphones) such that the total number of sensors in the \gls*{wasn} is $M\triangleq \sum_{k\in\K} M_k$. In the following, we consider the signals to be complex-valued to allow for frequency-domain representations, e.g., in the \gls*{stft} domain.
The signal $y_{k,m}[t]$ captured at time-frame (e.g., \gls*{stft} frame) $t$ by the $m$-th sensor of node $k$ is modelled as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:signal_model_single_sensor}
  y_{k,m}[t] =  s_{k,m}[t] + n_{k,m}[t],
\end{equation}

\noindent
where $s_{k,m}[t]$ and $n_{k,m}[t]$ represent the desired signal and the noise component, respectively. The noise component $n_{k,m}[t]$ may contain contributions from localized or diffuse noise sources as well as thermal microphone noise. For the sake of conciseness, the time index $[t]$ will be dropped in the following.
All sensor signals of node $k$ are stacked in the column vector $\mathbf{y}_k \triangleq  [y_{k,1},\dots,y_{k,M_k}]^\T\in\C[M_k]$, resulting in the multichannel signal model at node $k$:

% The signals $\mathbf{y}_k[t]\in\C[M_k]$ captured at time $t$ by the local sensors of node $k$ are modelled as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:signal_model}
  \yk =  \sk + \nk,
\end{equation}

\noindent
where $\sk$ and $\nk$ are defined in a similar way as $\yk$.
The desired signal component in~\eqref{eq:signal_model} can be further expanded as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:sk_to_slat}
  \sk =  \Psibk\mathbf{s}^\mathrm{lat},
\end{equation}

\noindent
where $\Psibk\in\C[M_k][S]$ is the steering matrix for node $k$ and $\mathbf{s}^\mathrm{lat}\in\C[S]$ is the latent signal produced by the desired sources.
Equation~\eqref{eq:sk_to_slat} can be interpreted as the frequency-domain equivalent of a time-domain convolution with an impulse response. Convolutive time-domain mixtures can thus be processed as instantaneous per-frequency mixtures in the (short-term) Fourier transform domain. Note that, in the latter case, the frame length must be chosen sufficiently large to fully capture the information of the underlying impulse response.
%It contains the transfer functions between each of the desired sources and each of the microphones at node $k$.

% \todoi{$Q=S$?}
% \todoi{Emphasize that this is an assumption and that, in practice, this does not exactly hold because a frame is shorter than a full RIR}

As in~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010}, we consider the node-specific signal estimation problem where each node $k\in\K$ aims at estimating its own $Q$-dimensional target signal $\dk$. This signal is a subset of the desired signal component in~\eqref{eq:signal_model}, such that:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:dk_to_sk}
  \dk \triangleq  \Ekk^\T\sk = \Psibkov \mathbf{s}^\mathrm{lat},
\end{equation}

% \todoi{Add practical example of this signal model in real applications (like in~\cite{szurley_topology-independent_2017}, below (5) there)}

\noindent
where $\Ekk\in\{0,1\}^{M_k\times Q}$ is a selection matrix and $\Psibkov \triangleq \Ekk^\T\Psibk\in\C[Q][S]$.
For \gls*{danse}, it was shown in~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010} that if $Q=S$ and $\Psibkov$ is a full-rank matrix, $\forall\:k\in\K$, the centralized \gls*{mwf} solution (as reviewed below) can be reached while nodes only broadcast $Q$-dimensional signals. In the following, it will be shown that this property also holds for \gls*{dansep}.
% 
%   it is assumed, for ease of exposition but without loss of generality, that $\dk$ is $Q$-dimensional, i.e.,
% } $\Ekk\in\{0,1\}^{M_k\times Q}$ is a selection matrix and $\Psibkov \triangleq \Ekk^\T\Psibk$ is a $Q\times Q$ matrix.
% This signal model assumes that node $k$ knows which of its sensor signal desired signal components it tries to estimate. Here, we also suppose that node $k$ aims at estimating the desired signal contribution of at most all its local sensors, and no linear combination thereof, i.e., $Q\leq M_k$.
% For the sake of a simpler exposition, it is assumed that $Q$ is equal for all nodes -- an extension to the general case where target signals at different nodes may have different dimensions is straightforward. %Finally, we assume $Q\geq S$, an established necessary assumption for convergence of \gls*{danse}~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010} and \gls*{tidanse}~\cite{szurley_topology-independent_2017}. As will be later exposed, this assumption is also necessary for the convergence of \gls*{dansep} but may be alleviated by use of \gls*{gevd}-based filter updates.
The target signal model from~\eqref{eq:dk_to_sk} is typical in a variety of practical scenarios, e.g., for a binaural hearing aid system striving to estimate a speech signal impinging on a single reference microphone in the left as well as in the right device while exploiting all microphone signals to improve the noise reduction performance~\cite{doclo_reduced-bandwidth_2009,cornelis_theoretical_2010}.

% Since the target signal definition from~\eqref{eq:dk_to_sk} holds for all nodes, it follows that the target signals between any two nodes can be related as:

% \begin{equation}\label{eq:dq_to_dk}
%   \dk[q] = \Psibkov[q](\Psibkov)^{-1}\dk,\:\forall\:(k,q)\in\K^2.
% \end{equation}

Considering the centralized case, where each node has access to all sensor signals in the \gls*{wasn}, the local sensor signal vectors from~\eqref{eq:signal_model} can be themselves stacked into a centralized signal vector $\mathbf{y}$ as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:yCentr}
  \mathbf{y} \triangleq  \begin{bmatrix}
    \yk[1]^\T \dots \yk[K]^\T
  \end{bmatrix}^\T.
\end{equation}

\noindent
Note that this vector can itself be split into similarly defined desired and noise components, denoted by $\mathbf{s}$ and $\mathbf{n}$, respectively. 
The objective for node $k$ is to estimate a node-specific filter $\Wk\in\C[M][Q]$ such that $\Wk^\Her\mathbf{y}$ is the best possible estimate of $\dk$.
A \gls*{lmmse} filter estimation problem is then considered, where the optimal filter $\hWk$ is obtained by solving:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:lmmseCentr}
  \hWk \triangleq  \underset{\mathbf{W}}{\mathrm{arg\,min}}\:
  \E[{
      \left\|
          \dk - \mathbf{W}^\Her\mathbf{y}
      \right\|^2_2
  }].
\end{equation}

\noindent
The solution of~\eqref{eq:lmmseCentr} is the \gls*{mwf}:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:centr_mwf}
    \hWk=\left(\Ryy\right)^{-1}\Rydk,
\end{equation}

\noindent
with the spatial covariance matrices (\glsunset{scm}\glspl*{scm}) $\Ryy\triangleq \mathbb{E}\{\mathbf{yy}^\Her\}$ and $\Rydk\triangleq \mathbb{E}\{\mathbf{yd}_k^\Her\}$. Estimating $\Ryy$ can be done by averaging over the available observations of $\mathbf{y}$, assuming short-term stationarity of the signals. Even though $\dk$ is unavailable at node $k$, $\Rydk$ can be estimated by first assuming that the desired and noise components in~\eqref{eq:signal_model} are uncorrelated for all $k\in\K$, which is often the case in, e.g., speech enhancement scenarios, giving:

\begin{align}\label{eq:Rydk_RssEk}
  \Rydk
  &= \E[\mathbf{y}\dk^\Her]
  = \E[\mathbf{s}\dk^\Her]\\
  &= \E[\mathbf{ss}^\Her]\Ek
  \triangleq  \Rss\Ek,\label{eq:Rydk_RssEk_final}
\end{align}

\noindent
While $\Rss$ itself cannot be directly observed, it can be indirectly estimated as $\Ryy - \Rnn$, where the noise \gls*{scm} $\Rnn \triangleq  \mathbb{E}\{\mathbf{nn}^\Her\}$ can, e.g., be estimated by making use of the on-off characteristics of the desired speech signal and a \gls*{vad} or by using prior knowledge~\cite{zhao2020model, dov2017multimodal, kim2018voice}. In practical online-processing applications, the \glspl*{scm} may be estimated using, e.g., exponential averaging as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:expavg}
  \begin{split}
    \Ryy[t] &= \beta\Ryy[t-1] + (1-\beta)\mathbf{y}[t]\mathbf{y}^\Her[t]\:\:\text{if VAD$[t]=1$},\\
    \Rnn[t] &= \beta\Rnn[t-1] + (1-\beta)\mathbf{y}[t]\mathbf{y}^\Her[t]\:\:\text{if VAD$[t]=0$},
  \end{split}
\end{equation}

\noindent
where 
% \Ryyh$ and $\Rnnh$ denote the estimated version of the true \glspl*{scm} $\Ryy$ and $\Rnn$, respectively, 
% $t$ is the time-frame index, 
$0\ll\beta<1$ is the forgetting factor and VAD$[t]$ denotes the \gls*{vad} value at time-frame $t$.


