\subsection{Algorithm Definition}\label{subsec:dansep_def}

In this section,
% we define \gls*{dansep} with consistent notation with respect to \gls*{danse}~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010}, while a slightly improved version is described in~\secref{subsec:implementation_aspects}. W
we define the \gls*{dansep} algorithm at a given iteration $i\in\N$, where one particular node $k(i)$ will update its local filters. The index $k(i)$ cycles over all nodes in a round-robin fashion\footnote{Non-sequential node-updating strategies such as simultaneous or asynchronous node-updating were developed for \gls*{danse} in~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010-1} but remain outside the scope of this paper. They have also not been proven to lead to convergence for \gls*{tidanse}~\cite{szurley_topology-independent_2017}.}. % The index of this updating node will change at every iteration. %The reader is invited to first review the present section, as it describes the entire algorithm in details.

At iteration $i$, the \gls*{wasn} is first pruned to a tree with root node $k(i)$, i.e., the updating node for iteration $i$ is the root node.  %Pruning to a tree can be done in various ways, as introduced in~\secref{subsec:graphs}. 
% Additionally, it will be shown in~\secref{subsec:pruning_strategy_discussion} that the choice of pruning strategy can have a significant impact on the convergence speed of \gls*{dansep} and that there exists a strategy that leads to maximally fast convergence.
A central aspect of \gls*{tidanse} is retained in \gls*{dansep} in that the pruned tree does not have to be the same at every iteration. %, unlike in \gls*{tdanse}~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2011_TDANSE}, where the tree must remain fixed throughout the entire estimation process.
In fact, an entirely new tree may be pruned at each iteration, consequently making \gls*{dansep} robust to dynamic \gls*{wasn} topologies and link failures. %Simulations validating this property are provided in~\secref{subsec:res_dyn}.
To account for the iteration-dependent tree, a superscript is added to the sets $\Uk[q]^i$ and $\Ukb[q]^i$ for all $q\in\K$.%, as they may be different at every iteration.
For the sake of notation simplicity, we omit the index $(i)$ in $k(i)$ in the following unless specified, keeping in mind that $k$ denotes the root node at iteration $i$.

Each node $q\in\K$ has a node-specific fusion matrix $\Pk[q]^i\in\C[M_q][Q]$ (defined later) which linearly combines its $M_q$ sensor signals into $Q$ fused signals as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:fused_signal_zk}
    \zkd[q]^i \triangleq  \Pk[q]^{i,\Her}\yk[q]\in\C[Q],\:\forall\:q\in\K.
\end{equation}

\noindent
In practice, iterations are spread over time such that each iteration $i$ uses a different frame of signal $\yk[q]$ to compute $\zkd[q]^i$.

%%%
\subsubsection{Fusion flow}\label{subsubsec:fusionflow}
%%%

After tree-pruning, a sum-and-send data flow starts at the leaf nodes and ends at the root node. This data flow is referred to as the \gls*{dansep} ``fusion flow''\footnote{\label{foot:semantic_tdanse}Semantic used in, e.g.,~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2011_TDANSE} to describe a similar data flow.}. Nodes receive partial in-network sums from their upstream neighbors and compute a new partial in-network sum including their own fused signals. The nodes then send the updated sum to their downstream neighbor. The partial in-network sum $\ettkq[q][l]^i$ sent from a leaf node or internal node $q\in\K\backslash\{k\}$ to its downstream neighbor $l$ is defined as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:fusionflow}
    \ettkq[q][l]^i
    \triangleq  \zkd[q]^i + \sum_{m\in\Uk[q]^i}\ettkq[m][q]^i
    = \zkd[q]^i + \sum_{m\in\Ukb[q]^i}\zkd[m]^i.
\end{equation}

% \noindent
% where $\zkd[m]^i$ denotes the fused signal that node $m$ has produced by linearly combining its sensor signals as in~\eqref{eq:fused_signal_zk}.
% The last step of the fusion flow is to build the complete in-network sum:

% \begin{equation}
%     \ettTI^i \triangleq  \zkdTI[r]^i + \sum_{q\in\Uk[r]}\ettkq[q][r]^i = \sum_{k\in\K}\zkdTI^i.
% \end{equation}
% This in-network sum is flooded back towards all other nodes in the \gls*{wasn} (referring to this as the ``diffusion flow''\footnote{See footnote~\ref{foot:semantic_tdanse}.} of \gls*{tidanse}) such that any node $k\in\K$ has access to the so-called observation vector:

% We consider the definition of the \gls*{dansep} algorithm at a given iteration $i\geq 0$.
% As was the case for \gls*{tidanse} (cf.~\secref{sec:tidanse_review}), the beginning of a new \gls*{dansep} iteration includes a pruning of the topology-unconstrained \gls*{wasn} to a tree with root $k$ as well as a fusion flow.

\noindent
Eventually, the root node $k$ has access to $|\Uk^i|$ partial in-network sums $\{\ettkq[l][k]^i\}_{l\in\Uk^i}$ received from its neighbors. Node $k$ then constructs the so-called \gls*{dansep} observation vector $\ty^i$ as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:yTildeRoot}
  \boxed{\ty^i \triangleq  \Big[
    \yk^\T \: \underbrace{\ettkq[l_1][k]^{i,\T}\:\dots\:\ettkq[l_B][k]^{i,\T}}_{\ztk^{i,\T}}
    \Big]^\T\in\C[{\tMk^i}],}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}\label{eq:tilde_Mk}
  \text{with}\:\:\tMk^i \triangleq M_k + Q|\Uk^i|,
\end{equation}

% \begin{align}\label{eq:yTildeRoot}
%   \ty^i &\triangleq  \Big[
%     \yk^\T \: \underbrace{\ettkq[q_1][k]^{i,\T}\:\dots\:\ettkq[q_B][k]^{i,\T}}_{\ztk^{i,\T}}
%     \Big]^\T\in\C[{\tMk^i}],\\
%   \text{with}\:\:\tMk^i &\triangleq M_k + Q|\Uk^i|,\label{eq:tilde_Mk}
% \end{align}

\noindent
where $\{l_1,\dots,l_B\}$ are the indices of the upstream neighbors of root node $k$, i.e., $\{l_1,\dots,l_B\}=\Uk^i$ and $B=|\Uk^i|$, and the partial in-network sums $\{\ettkq[l][k]^i\}_{l\in\Uk^i}$ can be stacked in a vector $\ztk^i\in\C[{Q|\Uk^i|}]$.
The observation vector in~\eqref{eq:yTildeRoot} for \gls*{dansep} represents a fundamental difference with \gls*{tidanse}, where the root node sums up the partial in-network sums $\{\ettkq[l][k]^i\}_{l\in\Uk^i}$ into a global in-network sum, i.e., $[
  \yk^\T\:|\: (\sum_{l\in\Uk^i}\ettkq[l][k]^i)^\T
]^\T = [
  \yk^\T\:|\: (\sum_{q\neq k}\zkd[q]^i)^\T
]^\T$. The number of \glspl*{dof} at the root node $k$ in \gls*{dansep} is thus $M_k + Q|\Uk^i|$, compared to $M_k + Q$ in \gls*{tidanse}. 
% From~\eqref{eq:tilde_Mk}, it also follows that the dimension of $\ty^i$, i.e., $\tMk^i$, depends on the pruned tree topology at iteration $i$ and thus may change at every iteration.
% The peer-to-peer fusion flow is depicted on~\figref{fig:fusionflow}.

The \gls*{dansep} fusion flow is depicted in~\figref{fig:fusionflow} in a topology-unconstrained \gls*{wasn} with $K=7$ nodes.

