The \gls*{dansep} diffusion flow is depicted in~\figref{fig:diffusionflow} in the same a topology-unconstrained \gls*{wasn} with $K=7$ nodes as~\figref{fig:fusionflow}.
The complete \gls*{dansep} algorithm is summarized in~\algref{alg:dansep}.

\begin{algorithm}%[h]
  \caption{\gls*{dansep}}\label{alg:dansep}
  \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \STATE Initialize $k \gets 1$.
    \STATE Initialize $\Wkk[q]^0$ and $\Pk[q]^0$ randomly, $\forall\:q\in\K$.
    \FOR{$i=1,2,3,\dots$}
      \STATE Prune \gls*{wasn} to tree with root node $k$.
      \STATE Each $q\in\K$ computes the fused signal $\zkd[q]^i$ as in~\eqref{eq:fused_signal_zk}.
      \STATE Fusion flow: partial in-network sums $\{\ettkq[l][k]\}_{l\in\Uk^i}$ are built via~\eqref{eq:fusionflow} and transmitted downstream towards the root node $k$.
      \STATE Root node $k$ builds $\ty^i = \begin{bmatrix}
        \yk^\T \: \ettkq[l_1][k]^{i,\T}\:\dots\:\ettkq[l_B][k]^{i,\T}
      \end{bmatrix}^\T$.
      \STATE Root node $k$ computes $\tW^{i+1}$ as in~\eqref{eq:DANSEp_mwf}.\label{algstep:Wtilde}
      \STATE Root node $k$ computes $\dhatk^{i+1}$ as in~\eqref{eq:dansep_dhat}.
      \STATE Diffusion flow: $\dhatk^{i+1}$ is transmitted upstream from the root node to all other nodes and, $\forall\:l\in\Uk^i$, $\Gkq[k][l]^{i+1}$ is transmitted upstream from the root node to all nodes in $\Ukb[l]^i\cup\{l\}$.
      \STATE All nodes update their fusion matrix via~\eqref{eq:PkDef}.
      \STATE Every non-root node $q$ computes $\dhatk[q]^{i+1}$ via~\eqref{eq:desSigEst}.
      \STATE Sequential node-updating: $k \gets (k+1)\mod K$.
    \ENDFOR
  \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% Variable-size observation vectors have not been exploited in any version of the \gls*{danse} algorithm, although were considered in~\cite{musluoglu_distributed_2023} for a distributed signal fusion problem where the resulting algorithm required all non-updating nodes to solve their own separate optimization problem. The \gls*{dansep} algorithm is computationally cheaper than the one proposed in~\cite{musluoglu_distributed_2023} as only the updating node solves an optimization problem.


In an \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn}, \gls*{dansep} can include the \gls*{danse} formulation as a particular case if, in each iteration, the \gls*{fc} network is pruned to a star topology with the updating node as hub. In that case,~\eqref{eq:yTildeRoot} becomes equivalent to the \gls*{danse} observation vector definition from~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010}. All non-root nodes are then leaf nodes and thus $\ettkq[q][k]^i = \zkd[q]^i,\:\forall\:q\in\K\backslash\{k\}$, giving:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:obsvectFC}
  \widetilde{\mathbf{y}}_{k,\mathrm{FC}}^i =  \begin{bmatrix}
    \yk^\T \: \zkd[1]^{i,\T} \:\dots\: \zkd[k-1]^{i,\T} \:\:\zkd[k+1]^{i,\T} \:\dots\:\zkd[K]^{i,\T}
  \end{bmatrix}^\T.
\end{equation}

\noindent
The \gls*{dansep} algorithm is, however, advantageous in terms of communication bandwidth with respect to \gls*{danse} in \gls*{fc} \glspl*{wasn} since \gls*{dansep} does not require the broadcasting of fused signals from all nodes in the \gls*{wasn} to every other node at each iteration. This aspect is discussed in~\secref{subsec:implementation_aspects}.

% it uses peer-to-peer data transmission (as the fusion flow only involves child-to-parent transmissions), which is cheaper in terms of communication bandwidth usage than the broadcasting scheme that \gls*{danse} relies on~\cite{bertrand_distributed_2010}.

% Furthermore, it can be noted that, although the iteration index is different between $\dhatk^i$ in~\eqref{eq:desSigEst} and $\dhatkTI^{i+1}$ in~\eqref{eq:dhatkTI}, they both are obtained from the same \textit{time-frame of data}, i.e., $\ty^i$ (resp. $\tyTI^i$). The different index is simply a notation decision that refers to the number of LMMSE updates conducted since the start of the algorithm to obtain the filters used to compute the corresponding target signal estimate. In \gls*{tidanse}, the filters computed using the data at frame $l$ are used to compute the target signal estimate at frame $l$ while, in DANSE$^+$, the computed computed using the data at frame $l-1$ are used to compute the target signal estimate at frame $l$. 


