\subsection{Tree-Pruning Strategy}\label{subsec:pruning_strategy_discussion}

% As mentioned in~\secref{subsec:graphs}, different ways exist to prune a connected \gls*{wasn} to a tree. 
The formulation of the \gls*{dansep} algorithm does not depend on a particular tree topology, such that the tree-pruning strategy may be freely chosen.
A typical choice is \glspl*{mst}, as they minimize the amount of energy required to transfer data through the tree by minimizing the total distance between connected nodes.
Although this is generally a popular option~\cite{szurley_topology-independent_2017}, there exists another strategy with its own advantages in the context of \gls*{dansep}, namely pruning the \gls*{wasn} such that $|\Uk^i|$ is maximized at any iteration $i$. %This will be justified in the following.

Indeed, it was shown in~\cite{szurley_topology-independent_2017} that the convergence speed of \gls*{danse}-like algorithms increases with the number of available \glspl*{dof} that an updating node possesses when solving its \gls*{lmmse} problem. This explains the slow convergence speed of \gls*{tidanse}, where the updating node only possesses $M_k+Q$ \glspl*{dof} compared to $M_k + Q(K-1)$ in \gls*{danse} for \gls*{fc} \glspl*{wasn}. In constract, the size of the \gls*{dansep} observation vector at the root node~\eqref{eq:yTildeRoot} is $\tMk^i = M_k + Q|\Uk^i|$. This implies that maximizing $|\Uk^i|$ at each iteration maximizes the number of \glspl*{dof} available at the root node which, in turns, maximizes the potential for a fast \gls*{dansep} convergence.

We propose a strategy to prune a topology-unconstrained \gls*{wasn} to a tree with a maximum $|\Uk^i|,\:\forall\:i$, and with a minimum total edge cost, which may be defined, e.g., as the Euclidean distance between nodes. This is essentially a constrained version of the Kruskal's \gls*{mst}-finding algorithm~\cite{kruskal1956shortest}.% and represents a trade-off between \gls*{dansep} convergence speed and communication cost.
The proposed tree-pruning strategy is termed \gls*{mmut} and is explicitly defined in~\algref{alg:mmut}. It considers the fact that the maximum number of connections that root node $k$ can have after pruning is the number of neighbors that $k$ has in the original non-pruned \gls*{wasn}. Examples of resulting tree topologies for \glspl*{wasn} are given in~\figref{fig:examples_pruning}. Note that applying \gls*{mmut} pruning to an \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn} will result in a star topology where all non-root nodes are directly connected to the root.

\begin{algorithm}%[h]
  \caption{\gls*{mmut} pruning}\label{alg:mmut}
  \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \STATE Let $\G$ denote the graph corresponding to a non-pruned topology-unconstrained \gls*{wasn} and $k$ the root node.
    \STATE Initialize tree $\Tree(k)$ with all edges of $\G$ connected to $k$.\label{algstep:mmut_step2}
    \STATE Sort the remaining edges by increasing weight (e.g., Euclidean distance between the two corresponding nodes) and list them accordingly in $\mathbf{e}_{\backslash k}$.
    \FOR{$e$ in $\mathbf{e}_{\backslash k}$ (in order of increasing weight)}
      \IF{$e\notin \Tree(k)$ \textbf{and} there is no path in $\Tree(k)$ between the nodes linked by $e$}
        \STATE Add $e$ to $\Tree(k)$.
      \ENDIF
    \ENDFOR\label{algstep:mmut_stepFinal}
  \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% \todoi{Improve MMUT (cf. Marc's last comments)? -- will increase added value w.r.t. EUSIPCO paper: idea is to maximize the \# of upstream neighbor at each node, so as to minimize the total \# of downstream hops.}

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=.9\columnwidth,trim={0 0 0 0},clip=false]{examples_pruning_v3.pdf}
  \caption{Examples of \gls*{mmut} applied to a topology-unconstrained \gls*{wasn} and an \gls*{fc} \gls*{wasn}. For the former, the first arrow points to the result of step~\ref{algstep:mmut_step2} in~\algref{alg:mmut}, while the second arrow points to the final pruned \gls*{wasn}, once step~\ref{algstep:mmut_stepFinal} is reached.}
  \label{fig:examples_pruning}
\end{figure}

To illustrate the potential of \gls*{mmut} pruning in the \gls*{dansep} context, a comparison in terms of (i) the number of neighbors at the root node $|\Uk|$ and (ii) the total pruned \gls*{wasn} edge length $E_k$ is made between trees resulting from~\algref{alg:mmut} and trees resulting from the Kruskal algorithm for \gls*{mst} pruning~\cite{kruskal1956shortest}.
% Quantities (i) and (ii) basically quantify the number of \gls*{dansep} \glspl*{dof} and the communication cost of the tree, respectively.%, and are denoted $|\Uk(\Tree(k))|$ and $E(\Tree(k))$, respectively. 

Random topology-unconstrained \glspl*{wasn} are created by setting a value for $K$ and generating a random set of node positions within a two-dimensional 5\,m $\times$ 5\,m space, ensuring a minimum distance of 10\,cm between every node. Connections are established between nodes within each other's communication radius, which is initialized at 1.5\,m and increased until a connected graph $\G$ is obtained. This graph is then pruned to a tree $\Tree(k)$ with root $k$ using \gls*{mst} pruning and \gls*{mmut} pruning. For every topology-unconstrained \glspl*{wasn}, $|\Uk|$ and $E_k$ are computed for all possible root node indices $k$ and the results are averaged into $|\mathcal{U}_\mathrm{avg}|$ and $E_\mathrm{avg}$, respectively. The comparison is performed over 200 randomly generated topology-unconstrained \glspl*{wasn}. Results are shown in~\figref{fig:pruning_comparison}.

\begin{figure}[h]
