The left-hand side plot in~\figref{fig:pruning_comparison} shows that \gls*{mmut} allows the average number of \gls*{dansep} \glspl*{dof} to consistently increase with $K$, while \gls*{mst} pruning results in an average of about two neighbors at the root node, independently of $K$. Linear regressions on the median values indicate that $|\mathcal{U}_\mathrm{avg}|$ grows with $0.18K$ for \gls*{mmut} pruning, against $0.03K$ (i.e., nearly independently of $K$) for \gls*{mst} pruning.

A trade-off in terms of communication cost is visible in the right-hand side plot. Linear regressions on the median values suggest that $E_\mathrm{avg}$ grows with $0.69K$ for \gls*{mmut} pruning against $0.59K$ for \gls*{mst} pruning. In other words, \gls*{mmut} pruning does incur a larger communication cost than \gls*{mst} pruning and this difference grows with $K$. This trade-off between convergence speed and communication cost is important to consider when designing solutions for particular scenarios. % We reiterate that, since the \gls*{dansep} algorithm formulation does not depend on the tree-pruning strategy, it is possible to dynamically adjust the latter according to, e.g., the instantaneous energy budget of the nodes.


