To assess the convergence properties of \gls*{dansep}, the 
\glspl*{scm} required to compute~\eqref{eq:DANSEp_mwf} are computed here via their true theoretical expressions.
% Note that, in practice, \glspl*{scm} are estimated using the available sensor data as described in~\secref{subsec:implementation_aspects}.
According to~\eqref{eq:Rss_theoretical}, the theoretical centralized desired signal \gls*{scm} is $\Rss = \Psibk\mathbf{R}_\mathbf{ss}^\mathrm{lat}\Psibk^\Her$ where $\mathbf{R}_\mathbf{ss}^\mathrm{lat}\in\C[S][S]$ is a diagonal matrix with the powers of the latent desired sources in $\mathbf{s}^\mathrm{lat}$ on the diagonal. Similar expressions can be obtained for $\Rnn$ and $\Ryy$. The \gls*{dansep} desired signal \gls*{scm} is obtained by transforming $\Rss$ as $\Rsst^i = \mathbf{C}_k^{i,\Her}\Rss\mathbf{C}_k^i$ where $\mathbf{C}_k^i\in\C[M][{\tMk^i}]$ contains an appropriate arrangement of the fusion matrices $\{\Pk[q]^i\}_{q\in\K\backslash\{k\}}$. The full expression for $\mathbf{C}_k^i$ can be found in Appendix C, alongside expressions for the matrices used to obtain the \gls*{danse} and \gls*{tidanse} desired signal \glspl*{scm} from $\Rss$.

Room impulse responses are computed for each source-sensor combination using the randomized image method~\cite{desenaModelingRectangularGeometries2015} adapted to 2-D spaces~\cite{scheiblerPyroomacousticsPythonPackage2018}, i.e., only taking planar reflections from walls into account. The impulse responses are then truncated to the frame size $L$ to fulfill the signal model assumption~\eqref{eq:sk_to_slat}. Their \gls*{dft} of length $L$ is computed and, for each frequency bin, the resulting frequency responses are stacked appropriately to obtain the centralized \glspl*{scm}. All other \glspl*{scm} (for \gls*{danse}, \gls*{tidanse}, and \gls*{dansep}) are computed based on the centralized \glspl*{scm} and the corresponding sets of fusion matrices. %In the following, results are shown only for processing in the 20\textsuperscript{th} frequency bin (arbitrarily chosen).

% \todoi{$\uparrow$ to adapt for wideband processing with speech}

% The signal samples of $s^\mathrm{lat}$ and $\mathbf{n}^\mathrm{lat}$ are drawn from the uniform distribution over $[-0.5, 0.5]$. The steering matrix entry between any source $s$ and any sensor $m$ at any node $q\in\K$ is $[\Psibk[q]]_{m,s} = 1/d_{sm}$, where $d_{sm}$ is the Euclidean distance between $s$ and $m$, which corresponds to a free-field environment.

The convergence speed of \gls*{dansep} is compared to that of \gls*{tidanse} and \gls*{danse} through three metrics, each computed at every iteration. Firstly, the \gls*{snr} at iteration $i$ is computed from the entire time-domain version $\dhatk^i[t]$ of the estimated target signal as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:snr}
  \mathrm{SNR}^i \triangleq \frac{1}{K}
  \sum_{k=1}^K
  10\log_{10}\left(
    \frac{\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left\|\dhatk^i[t]\right\|_2^2}{\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left\|\nhatk^i[t]\right\|_2^2}
  \right),
\end{equation}

\noindent
where $\nhatk^i[t]$ is the time-domain version of the  post-filtering noise-only contribution to the estimated target signal at node $k$ and iteration $i$, i.e., from the decomposition $\dhatk^i[t]=\shatk^i[t]+\nhatk^i[t]$ (see~\eqref{eq:signal_model}), and $T$ is the number of time-domain samples (given the simulation conditions, $T=32\cdot10^4$).

Secondly, the \gls*{stoi}~\cite{taalAlgorithmIntelligibilityPrediction2011} is computed over the entire time-domain version $\dhatk^i[t]$ of the estimated target signal, taking the true target signal as reference.

Thirdly, the \gls*{mse} between the network-wide filters\footnote{For mathematical expressions of the network-wide filters, the reader is referred to~\eqref{eq:nw_filter_dansep} for \gls*{dansep}, \cite[pp.8]{bertrand_distributed_2010} for \gls*{danse}, and~\cite[pp.6]{szurley_topology-independent_2017} for \gls*{tidanse}.} $\Wk[q]^{i}$ at iteration $i$ and the centralized filters $\hWk[q]$ from~\eqref{eq:centr_mwf} is computed. It is defined as:

\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{MSE}_{W}^i \triangleq \frac{1}{K} \sum_{q=1}^K \left\|
    \Wk[q]^{i} - \hWk[q]
  \right\|^2_\mathrm{F}.
\end{equation}

\noindent
Note that, since processing is conducted in the \gls*{stft}-domain, the filters $\Wk[q]^{i}$ and $\hWk[q]$ are frequency-dependent, i.e., they are computed for each frequency bin separately. The \gls*{mse} is thus computed for each frequency bin and averaged across all bins to obtain a single value per iteration $i$.

For different sensing environments, the relative node-to-source positions are different and so are the steering matrices. The convergence profile of the algorithms in terms of $\mathrm{MSE}_{W}^i$ can thus be expected to differ between sensing environments. In order to unambiguously depict the average $\mathrm{MSE}_{W}^i$ values obtained across multiple sensing environments, we plot a geometric mean on a logarithmic axis, defined as:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:geommean}
  \overline{\mathrm{MSE}}_{W}^i \triangleq \left(
    \prod_{m=1}^{N_\mathrm{SE}} \mathrm{MSE}_{W}^i(m)
  \right)^{\frac{1}{N_\mathrm{SE}}},
\end{equation}

\noindent
where $\mathrm{MSE}_{W}^i(m)$ denotes the value obtained for the $m$-th sensing environment at iteration $i$. The difference between~\eqref{eq:geommean} and a simple arithmetic mean is depicted through an example in~\figref{fig:res_justification_geomean}, where both are computed for the \gls*{danse} algorithm in 50 randomly generated environments with \gls*{fc} \glspl*{wasn}. Steeper slopes indicate faster convergence, i.e., the network-wide filters converge to the centralized filters in fewer iterations. It can be seen that the geometric mean captures more unambiguously the trend of the \gls*{mse} on a logarithmic axis. This is because the arithmetic mean is primarily influenced by the few cases where $\mathrm{MSE}_{W}^i(m)$ is slowly decreasing, even though most sensing environments have led to faster convergence. It should be noted that the \gls*{snr} from~\eqref{eq:snr} and \gls*{stoi} are still averaged across sensing environments using the arithmetic mean.

