\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:ccl}

% In this paper, the \gls*{dansep} algorithm was introduced to perform distributed \gls*{lmmse}-based node-specific signal estimation in topology-unconstrained \glspl*{wasn}, while maintaining quick convergence towards the centralized \gls*{mwf} estimate. A tree-pruning strategy was also proposed to maximize the convergence speed of \gls*{dansep}. The potential of \gls*{dansep} as single-algorithm alternative to a combination of \gls*{danse} and \gls*{tidanse} was established by showing that \gls*{dansep} not only outperforms \gls*{tidanse} in terms of convergence speed towards the centralized solution in any non-\gls*{fc} topology, but also matches the convergence speed of \gls*{danse} in \gls*{fc} networks.
% Main conclusions:
% \begin{itemize}
%   \item \gls*{dansep} can be used to replace \gls*{tidanse} / \gls*{danse} and get: better convergence speed + less exchanged data.
%   \item Tree-pruning strategy is a crucial parameter for the convergence speed of \gls*{dansep}.
%   \item Challenges remain: online-mode \glspl*{scm} estimation $\to$ requires reset of \glspl*{scm} at every $i$ $\to$ still a bit unrealistic $\to$ \gls*{scm} estimation should be a focus in future work (DNN-based solutions)?
% \end{itemize}

We proposed \gls*{dansep}, a distributed \gls*{lmmse}-based algorithm for node-specific signal estimation in topology-unconstrained \glspl*{wasn}. The algorithm achieves rapid convergence toward the centralized \gls*{mwf} estimate without requiring a fully connected (\gls*{fc}) topology. To increase convergence speed, a tree-pruning strategy was introduced that maximizes the number of neighbors at the updating node.

The results demonstrate that \gls*{dansep} serves as an effective single-algorithm replacement for the combined use of \gls*{danse} and \gls*{tidanse}. It consistently outperforms \gls*{tidanse} in non-\gls*{fc} networks and matches the convergence speed of \gls*{danse} in \gls*{fc} topologies, while significantly reducing the amount of data exchanged between nodes in that case. The choice of tree-pruning strategy is shown to be a key factor in achieving these improvements, as it directly influences the rate of convergence.

However, challenges remain, particularly with respect to online operation. The \gls*{dansep} algorithm, in the same way as \gls*{danse} and \gls*{tidanse}, requires the spatial covariance matrices (\glspl*{scm}) needed for filter update to be re-estimated at each iteration, which limits its practical applicability in scenarios requiring high-speed adaptivity. Attempting to bridge this gap is a promising direction for future work towards the deployment of \gls*{dansep} in real-world \glspl*{wasn}.

