To calculate the homology of a simplicial complex, we use the Betti numbers, which indicate how many holes of each dimension an object has. The Betti number is a topological invariant, which means it does not change under continuous transformations. Because these numbers are independent of the topology of the space (the exact position of points may not matter, only their relative positions in relation to each other), so,  we can study the topology of a space without explicit geometric description.  Take the torus:
