\section{Log Depth Enables Recognizing Regular Languages}
\label{sec:reglangs}

Constant-depth transformers cannot recognize regular languages, a natural task closely related to state tracking \citep{liu2023transformers,merrill2024illusion}.
\citet[Theorem 1]{liu2023transformers} show that a non-uniform variant of log-depth transformer can recognize regular languages using an associative prefix-sum construction \citep[cf.][]{hillis1986data,BlellochTR90}.
However, it is unclear whether standard transformers can implement their construction \citep[Page 44]{liu2023transformers} for two key reasons:
\begin{compactenum}
    \item Their construction is \emph{non-uniform}, in the sense that the parameters depend on the input length and depth of the network. This means that it is unclear whether a single transformer could implement it in a way that generalizes to inputs of any length.

    \item Their result requires two strong simplifications to the transformer architecture: removing residual connections and assuming specific, nonstandard positional encodings. As discussed in \Cref{subsec:memory-management}, dealing with residual connections is particularly tricky in universal transformers, requiring proper memory management of cells in the residual stream so that outputs from the previous iteration of a layer do not interfere with a later iteration.
\end{compactenum}
Our result, leveraging \Cref{lem:division} (full proof in \Cref{proof:reglangs}), addressed both of these weaknesses. A single transformer with \emph{fixed parameters} can recognize strings of \emph{any length}; moreover this transformer can have residual connections and does not rely on specific positional encodings:

\begin{restatable}[Regular Language Recognition]{theorem}{reglangs} \label{thm:reg-langs}
    Let $L$ be a regular language over $\Sigma$ and $\$ \not\in \Sigma$.
    Then there exists a $(0,8,9)$-universal transformer with causal masking
    % of size $\O(k\abs{M}^2)$
    that, on any string $w \$$, recognizes whether $w \in L$ when unrolled to $\ceil{\log_2 \abs{w}}$ depth.
\end{restatable}

