% This visual demonstration of DT synchronization is further supported by quantitative metrics like Trajectory Discrepancy ($L_{traj}$), which directly evaluates the fidelity of our perception and tracking pipeline within this controlled environment. While vehicle movements within the CARLA environment are precisely governed by the same ground-truth trajectories ($t_s$) provided by the SUMO DT, our lane detection and tracking module processes the visual output from CARLA to derive detected trajectories ($t_c$). Therefore, $L_{traj}$ quantifies the discrepancy arising from the perception system's ability to accurately extract and track vehicle movements from the rendered visual data, serving as a direct measure of our model's tracking performance against a known ground truth within a controlled simulation. This combined qualitative and quantitative assessment underscores the operational viability and potential of our DT-integrated framework for real-time ITS applications.

