 The proposed privacy-preserving, verifiable LLM framework \textsc{ZKPROV} comprises $3$ main algorithms that work together to establish and verify the required verification chain. 

 \textsc{ZKPROV} = \{\textsc{SETUP}, \textsc{PROVE}, \textsc{VERIFY} \} as defined in the following.

\begin{itemize}

\item \(\textsc{Setup}(1^\lambda, W_0) \rightarrow (C, \Omega)\): Given security parameter \(\lambda\) and base model \(W_0\), generates the necessary cryptographic parameters for all participants, performs dataset authentication and model fine-tuning, and produces public commitments \(C\) and private witnesses \(\Omega\) for all sensitive protocol components.

    \item  \(\textsc{Prove}(C, \Omega, p) \rightarrow (r, \pi)\): Given commitments \(C\), witnesses \(\Omega\), and user prompt \(p\), selects the optimal dataset, generates response \(r\) using the corresponding model, and produces a proof \(\pi\) demonstrating that the response derives from a model trained on authenticated data.

    \item \(\textsc{Verify}(p, r, \pi, C) \rightarrow \{\textsc{Accept}, \textsc{Reject}\}\): Given prompt \(p\), response \(r\), proof \(\pi\), and commitments \(C\), validates all cryptographic relationships to confirm that the response was derived from a model trained on the authenticated datasets without learning sensitive information about dataset contents or model parameters.

\end{itemize}

\vspace{-5mm}
\subsection{Detailed Description of ZKPROV}
\label{detail}

This section provides proposed ZKPROV scheme's details, comprising setup and preprocessing, proof construction, and verification phases. 
\vspace{-3mm}
\subsubsection{Setup and Preprocessing}
\label{phase1}
The authority $\mathcal{CA}$ generates keys and public parameters of the framework and distributes them among the parties to be the inputs of all the components. It generates the common reference string (\text{crs}) that contains domain separators \(\kappa_1\), \(\kappa_2\), and \(\kappa_3\) that ensure cryptographic separation between different protocol components, preventing collision attacks across operational domains.

$\mathcal{CA}$ stores the datasets in a Reckle Tree $T_i$ (Sec.~\ref{tree}) and their roots  $\rho_i$ as commitments to the entire dataset. For each dataset, the authority constructs metadata \(m_i = (\rho_i, \text{Att}_i, \text{id}_i)\) that combines the $\rho_i$ with semantic attributes and administrative identifiers. The authority then generates BLS signatures \(\sigma_i\) (Sec.~\ref{BLS}), on each metadata package \(m_i\) using their secret key \(sk_\mathcal{CA}\), creating unforgeable authentication tokens that cryptographically bind each dataset to its authorized usage constraints.  They transmit the authentication package $AP = (pp, D_i, m_i, \sigma_i, T_i)$ to the  $\mathcal{P}$.

\(\mathcal{P}\) receives $AP$ from the authority and verifies the signature using the $\mathcal{CA}$'s public key to ensure dataset authenticity. If the verification is successful, the \(\mathcal{P}\) fine-tunes the base model \(W_0\) using their specified hyperparameters \(H = (\eta, B, E, O)\) to obtain dataset-specific weights
$W_i \leftarrow \textsc{FINETUNE}(D_i, W_0, H).$
The provider then computes layer-wise weight differences \(\Delta W_{i,j} = W_{i,j} - W_{0,j}\) for each layer \(j \in \{1, \ldots, N\}\), capturing the specific transformations on dataset \(D_i\). 
%These weight differences form the foundation of the binding mechanism to cryptographically link model responses to their training datasets

%Then the \(\mathcal{P}\) creates commitments to all sensitive protocol components using randomness values that will later serve dual purposes in both hiding the committed values and enabling deterministic challenge vector generation.

For each dataset, the provider generates commitments to the authorities signatures and metadata by calling \(C_{\sigma,i} \leftarrow \textsc{C.COMMIT}(\sigma_i, \omega_{\sigma,i})\) and \(C_{m_i} \leftarrow \textsc{C.COMMIT}(m_i, \omega_{m_i})\) for the signature and metadata respectively, along with commitments to the weight differences \(C_{\Delta W,i} \leftarrow \textsc{C.COMMIT}(\Delta W_i, \omega_{\Delta W,i})\) and the base model \(C_{W_0} \leftarrow \textsc{C.COMMIT}(W_0, \omega_{W_0})\). The randomness values \(\omega_{\sigma,i}\) and \(\omega_{m_i}\) serve a critical role beyond standard commitment hiding, as they are used to generate the seed value \(\text{seed}_i = \textsc{PRF}(\omega_{\sigma,i} \parallel \omega_{m_i} \parallel \kappa_2)\) that ensures the following challenge vectors can only be computed by solely possessing valid commitments to authenticated $m_i$'s.

For each dataset \(i\) and layer \(j\), the provider computes challenge vectors \(v_{i,j} = \textsc{Hash}(\text{seed}_i \parallel j \parallel \kappa_1)\), where the seed incorporates the commitment randomness and the domain separator \(\kappa_2\) ensures contextual uniqueness across different datasets. The $v_{i,j}$ generation mechanism maintains the non-interactive nature of the zero-knowledge proof system while ensuring unpredictability properties essential for security. This construction guarantees that $v_{i,j}$ are deterministically derivable during proof generation and verification, yet remain computationally unpredictable to any $\mathcal{P}$ who does not possess the correct signature on authenticated $m_i$ and the corresponding $\omega_{m_i}$. The layer-specific domain separator \(\kappa_1\) ensures that different layers produce distinct challenge vectors even for the same dataset, preventing cross-layer collision attacks. 

The binding computation creates cryptographic fingerprints that uniquely identify the weight differences through inner product evaluations. For each dataset \(i\) and layer \(j\), the provider calculates 

$B_{i,j} \leftarrow \langle \Delta W_{i,j}, \mathbf{v}_{i,j} \rangle = \sum_{k=1}^{d} \Delta W_{i,j}[k] \cdot \mathbf{v}_{i,j}[k].$

Where \(d\) represents the dimensionality of the weight vectors in layer \(j\). These binding values are cryptographically sound under the Schwartz-Zippel lemma \cite{schwartz1980fast}, which guarantees that distinct multivariate polynomials evaluated at random points yield different results with overwhelming probability. The provider then commits to these binding values as \(C_{B,i} \leftarrow \textsc{C.COMMIT}(\{B_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^N, \omega_{B,i})\).

The final commitment vector hides the actual values \(m_i, \sigma_i, \Delta W_{i,j}, B_{i,j},\) and \(W_0\) while enabling their use within the zero-knowledge proofs generation mechanism. 
$C = (C_{m_i}, C_{\sigma,i}, C_{\Delta W,i}, C_{B,i}, C_{W_0})$

The challenge vectors \(\mathbf{v}_{i,j}\) are not explicitly committed to as part of this public set \({C}\), as their correct derivation and usage is proven internally during the ZK proof based on the witnesses that open \(C_{m_i}\) and \(C_{\sigma,i}\). This design choice reduces the public commitment size while maintaining the ability to verify that challenge vectors were computed correctly using authenticated inputs. The provider stores all commitment opening information \(\Omega = (\omega_{m_i}, \omega_{\sigma,i}, \omega_{\Delta W,i}, \omega_{B,i}, \omega_{W_0})\) as private witnesses to be used during proof generation to demonstrate knowledge of the committed values and their relationships as showin in Alg.~\ref{alg1}.

\subsubsection{Proof Construction}
\label{proofconstruct}

The user \(\mathcal{U}\) submits a prompt \(p\), triggering the embedded retrieval algorithm that selects the optimal dataset. The model provider identifies the most relevant dataset by calling
$D_{i^*} \leftarrow \textsc{MATCH}(p, \text{Att}_p, \{(D_i, \text{Att}_i)\}_{i=1}^m).$

where the matching function evaluates the prompt attributes \(\text{Att}_p\) against the dataset attributes \(\{\text{Att}_i\}_{i=1}^m\) to identify the most contextually relevant dataset. This selection process ensures that the response is generated using the model weights \(W_{i^*}\) that were specifically trained on the dataset most appropriate for the query domain. For simplicity and avoiding complex notation, we refer to the selected dataset index as \(i\) (i.e., \(i \leftarrow i^*\)) for the remainder of the proof construction process.

\vspace{-4mm}
\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Setup and Preprocessing Phase of ZKPROV}
\label{alg1}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{\textsc{SETUP}}{$\{D_i\}_{i=1}^m, W_0, \text{crs}$}

    The $\mathcal{CA}$ generates $pp$, and authenticates datasets:
    \For{$i = 1$ to $m$}


        \State Generates $AP = (D_i, m_i, \sigma_i, T_i)$ to $\mathcal{P}$
    \EndFor

    The $\mathcal{P}$ verifies authentication and fine-tunes the model:
    \For{$i = 1$ to $m$}

        \If{ Signature $\sigma_i$ on $ m_i$ is valid}
            \State $W_i \gets \textsc{FINETUNE}(D_i, W_0, H)$
            \For{$j = 1$ to $N$}
                \State $\Delta W_{i,j} \gets W_{i,j} - W_{0,j}$
            \EndFor
        \EndIf
    \EndFor

    The $\mathcal{P}$ generates commitments:
    \State $\omega_{W_0} \stackrel{\$}{\leftarrow} \mathbb{F}_p$
    \State $C_{W_0} \gets \textsc{C.COMMIT}(W_0, \omega_{W_0})$

    \For{$i = 1$ to $m$}
        \State $\omega_{\sigma,i}, \omega_{m,i}, \omega_{\Delta W,i}, \omega_{B,i} \stackrel{\$}{\leftarrow} \mathbb{F}_p$
        \State $C_{\sigma,i} \gets \textsc{C.COMMIT}(\sigma_i, \omega_{\sigma,i})$
        \State $C_{m,i} \gets \textsc{C.COMMIT}(m_i, \omega_{m,i})$
        \State $C_{\Delta W,i} \gets \textsc{C.COMMIT}(\Delta W_i, \omega_{\Delta W,i})$
    \EndFor

    The $\mathcal{P}$ generates challenge vectors and binding values:
    \For{$i = 1$ to $m$}
        \State $\text{seed}_i \gets \textsc{PRF}(\omega_{\sigma,i} || \omega_{m,i} || \kappa_2)$
        \For{$j = 1$ to $N$}
            \State $v_{i,j} \gets \textsc{Hash}(\text{seed}_i || j || \kappa_1)$
            \State $B_{i,j} \leftarrow \langle \Delta W_{i,j}, v_{i,j} \rangle$
        \EndFor
        \State $C_{B,i} \gets \textsc{C.COMMIT}(\{B_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^N, \omega_{B,i})$
    \EndFor

    The $\mathcal{CA}$ assembles the final commitments and witnesses:
    \State $\mathcal{C} \gets \{C_{m,i}, C_{\sigma,i}, C_{\Delta W,i}, C_{B,i}\}_{i=1}^m \cup \{C_{W_0}\}$
    \State $\Omega \gets \{\omega_{m,i}, \omega_{\sigma,i}, \omega_{\Delta W,i}, \omega_{B,i}\}_{i=1}^m \cup \{\omega_{W_0}\}$

    \State \textbf{Return} $(\mathcal{C}, \Omega)$
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\vspace{-5mm}

The $\mathcal{P}$ utilizes the model weights corresponding to the selected dataset and produces the LLM output $r = \textsc{LLM}(W_{i}, p)$

Using the dataset-specific model weights, ensuring that the response reflects the knowledge patterns embedded during fine-tuning on the authenticated dataset \(D_i\). Since the response \(r\) is known to the verifier after transmission, it can be directly incorporated into the cryptographic transcript that binds the entire query-response interaction to the specific dataset used for generation. The provider creates a query transcript $\tau \leftarrow \textsc{Hash}(C_{m_i} \parallel p \parallel r \parallel \kappa_3).$

where \(\kappa_3\) is the query binding separator defined in the $crs$. This transcript serves as a public digest that succinctly binds the committed dataset metadata, the user $p$, and the generated $r$ into a single cryptographic identifier that can be verified without revealing the underlying sensitive information.

The ZK proof construction requires demonstrating knowledge of valid openings for all commitments while proving the correctness of multiple relationships. The provider must prove that the committed metadata \(m_i\) carries an authentic signature from authority \(\mathcal{CA}\), that the challenge vectors \(\mathbf{v}_{i,j}\) were correctly derived from the authenticated metadata and commitment randomness, and that the binding formula $B_{i,j}$ is computed correctly for each layer \(j\). Additionally, the proof must establish that the weight differences \(\Delta W_{i,j}\) are consistent with the model \(W_i\) derived from the base model \(W_0\) and dataset \(D_i\), and that the entire process aligns with the public query transcript \(\tau\).

The signature authenticity proof demonstrates that the committed signature \(\sigma_i\) is valid for the committed metadata \(m_i\) under the authority's public key. The provider constructs a zero-knowledge proof. 
\[
\pi_{\sigma} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.PROVE}(pp_{zk}, (C_{m_i}, C_{\sigma,i}, pk_\mathcal{CA}), (\sigma_i, m_i, \omega_{m_i}, \omega_{\sigma,i}))
\]

The proof ensures that only responses derived from models trained on authenticated datasets can be validated, preventing the use of unauthorized or tampered training data.


The binding correctness proof demonstrates that each binding value \(B_{i,j}\) was computed as the correct inner product between the committed weight differences and the challenge vectors derived from the authenticated metadata. 

The provider leverages HyperNova's recursive folding mechanism to aggregate all layer-wise binding constraints into a single succinct proof. The folding process begins with an empty state and iteratively incorporates each layer's binding constraint through \[
S_{B,j} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.FOLD}(S_{B,j-1}, R_{B,i,j}, \omega_{B,j}).
\] Where \(R_{B,i,j}\) represents the complete relation including both the inner product computation and the challenge vector derivation, and \(\omega_{B,j} = (\Delta W_{i,j}, B_{i,j}, \omega_{\Delta W,i}, \omega_{B,i}, \omega_{m,i}, \omega_{\sigma,i})\) contains all necessary witness values. After processing all \(N\) layers, the provider generates the final recursive proof that succinctly demonstrates the correctness of all binding computations and challenge vector derivations with constant verification complexity.\[
\pi_{B}^{rec} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.PROVE}(S_{B,N}).
\] 


The proof of weight consistency establishes that the committed weight differences correctly represent the transformation from the base model to the model for each layer. Since we have layer-wise weight differences \(\Delta W_{i,j}\) for \(j \in \{1, \ldots, N\}\), the provider must use recursive folding to prove the relation $\Delta W_{i,j} = W_{i,j} - W_{0,j}$

Holds for all layers simultaneously. 

The recursive weight difference proof begins with an empty state and iteratively incorporates each layer's weight consistency constraint through 
\[
S_{\Delta,j} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.FOLD}(S_{\Delta,j-1}, R_{\Delta,i,j}, \omega_{\Delta,j}),
\]
where \(R_{\Delta,i,j}\) represents the relation \(\Delta W_{i,j} = W_{i,j} - W_{0,j}\) and \(\omega_{\Delta,j} = (W_{0,j}, W_{i,j}, \Delta W_{i,j}, \omega_{W_0}, \omega_{W,i}, \omega_{\Delta W,i})\) contains the layer-specific witness values and opening randomness. 

After processing all \(N\) layers, the provider generates the final recursive weight consistency proof 
\[
\pi_{\Delta}^{rec} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.PROVE}(S_{\Delta,N}),
\]
That prevents malicious providers from claiming that arbitrary weight differences correspond to legitimate fine-tuning processes.

The query transcript consistency proof links the entire provenance chain to the specific query-response interaction, demonstrating that the committed dataset metadata used in the proof corresponds to the dataset selection for the given prompt and response. The provider shows that the query transcript \(\tau = \textsc{Hash}(C_{m_{i}} \parallel p \parallel r \parallel \kappa_3)\) was correctly computed using the committed metadata for the selected dataset, the user's prompt, and the generated response. This proof $$\pi_{\tau} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.PROVE}(pp_{zk}, (C_{m,i}, p, r, \tau), (m_i, \omega_{m_i}))$$ ensures that the provenance verification is specific to the actual query-response pair rather than a generic proof that could be reused across different interactions.

The final proof aggregation combines all individual proof components into a single succinct zero-knowledge proof that can be efficiently verified. The provider assembles \(\pi \leftarrow (\pi_{\sigma}, \pi_{B}^{rec}, \pi_{\Delta}, \pi_{\tau})\), containing all necessary proof elements to validate the complete provenance chain. This aggregated proof demonstrates that the provider knows secret witnesses that correctly open all public commitments and satisfy all underlying cryptographic relationships, while maintaining zero-knowledge properties that prevent the verifier from learning any information about the dataset contents, model parameters, or internal computational details beyond what is necessary for provenance verification. We provide a summary of the interactions in the Alg. \ref{alg2}


\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Proof Construction Phase of ZKPROV}
\label{alg2}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{\textsc{Prove}}{$C, \Omega, p$}
\State \textbf{Input:} Commitments \(C\), Witnesses \(\Omega\), Prompt \(p\)
%\State \textbf{Output:} Response \(r\), Proof \(\pi\)

The $\mathcal{P}$ constructs the proofs:
\State \(i^* \leftarrow \textsc{MATCH}(p, \text{Att}_p, \{(D_i, \text{Att}_i)\}_{i=1}^m)\)
\State \(i \leftarrow i^* \) 
\State \(r \leftarrow \textsc{LLM}(W_i, p)\)

\State \(\tau \leftarrow \textsc{Hash}(C_{m,i} \parallel p \parallel r \parallel \kappa_3)\)

\State \(\text{witness}_\sigma \leftarrow (\sigma_i, m_i, \omega_{m,i}, \omega_{\sigma,i})\)
\State \(\text{public}_\sigma \leftarrow (C_{m,i}, C_{\sigma,i}, pk_A)\)
\State \(\pi_\sigma \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.PROVE}(pp_{zk}, \text{public}_\sigma, \text{witness}_\sigma)\)
\State \(S_{B,0} \leftarrow \emptyset\)
\For{$j = 1$ to $N$}

    \State \(R_{B,i,j} \leftarrow (\text{seed}_i = \textsc{PRF}(\omega_{\sigma,i} \parallel \omega_{m,i} \parallel \kappa_2) \wedge\)
    \State \(\quad v_{i,j} = \textsc{Hash}(\text{seed}_i \parallel j \parallel \kappa_1) \wedge\)
    \State \(\quad B_{i,j} = \langle \Delta W_{i,j}, v_{i,j}\rangle \)
    \State \(\omega_{B,j} \leftarrow (\Delta W_{i,j}, B_{i,j}, \omega_{\Delta W,i}, \omega_{B,i}, \omega_{m,i}, \omega_{\sigma,i})\)
    \State \(S_{B,j} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.FOLD}(S_{B,j-1}, R_{B,i,j}, \omega_{B,j})\)
\EndFor
\State \(\pi_B^{rec} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.PROVE}(S_{B,N})\)
\State \(S_{\Delta,0} \leftarrow \emptyset\)
\For{$j = 1$ to $N$}
    \State \(R_{\Delta,i,j} \leftarrow (\Delta W_{i,j} = W_{i,j} - W_{0,j})\)
    \State \(\text{witness}_{\Delta,j} \leftarrow (W_{0,j}, W_{i,j}, \Delta W_{i,j}, \omega_{W_0}, \omega_{W,i}, \omega_{\Delta W,i})\)
    \State \(S_{\Delta,j} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.FOLD}(S_{\Delta,j-1}, R_{\Delta,i,j}, \text{witness}_{\Delta,j})\)
\EndFor
\State \(\pi_\Delta^{rec} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.PROVE}(S_{\Delta,N})\)


\State \(\text{witness}_\tau \leftarrow (m_i, \omega_{m_i})\)
\State \(\text{public}_\tau \leftarrow (C_{m_i}, p, r, \tau)\)
\State \(\pi_\tau \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.PROVE}(pp_{zk}, \text{public}_\tau, \text{witness}_\tau)\)

\State \(\pi \leftarrow (\pi_\sigma, \pi_B^{rec}, \pi_\Delta^{rec}, \pi_\tau)\)

\State \textbf{Return} \((r, \pi)\)
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsubsection{Verification phase} 
\label{verification}

The user \(\mathcal{U}\) receives the response \(r\) and the aggregated proof \(\pi \) from the provider, along with the public commitments \(C\), the query transcript \(\tau\), and validates each component of the provenance chain to ensure that all cryptographic relationships hold correctly and that the response genuinely originates from a model trained on authority-authenticated data.


The signature authenticity verification confirms that the committed signature \(\sigma_i\) is valid for the committed metadata \(m_i\) under the authority's public key \(pk_{\mathcal{CA}}\). The verifier executes 
\[
\{0,1\} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.VERIFY}(pp_{zk}, (C_{m,i}, C_{\sigma,i}, pk_{\mathcal{CA}}), \pi_{\sigma})
\]
\noindent to ensure that the dataset used for training was properly authenticated by the designated authority rather than being an unauthorized or tampered dataset.

Then the \(\mathcal{U}\) validates the recursive binding among all the layers, ensuring their correct computation using challenge vectors derived from authenticated metadata. The verifier calls 
\[
\{0,1\} \leftarrow {ZK.VERIFY}(pp_{zk}, S_{B,N}, \pi_{B}^{rec}),
\]
where \(S_{B,N}\) represents the final folded state containing all \(N\) binding constraints. This verification confirms that each binding value is computed as the correct inner product of  $\Delta W_{i,j}$ and ${v}_{i,j}$. Since this proof is generated recursively using the folding mechanism of the HyperNova proof system, its verification is constant-time regardless of the number of model layers, making the system scalable to large language models with hundreds of layers.

The recursive weight consistency verification ensures that the committed weight differences correctly represent the transformation from the base model to the model across all layers. The verifier executes $\{0,1\} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.VERIFY}(pp_{zk}, S_{\Delta,N}, \pi_{\Delta}^{rec})$

to validate that the relation \(\Delta W_{i,j} = W_{i,j} - W_{0,j}\) holds for all layers \(j \in \{1, \ldots, N\}\). This verification prevents malicious providers from claiming that arbitrary weight differences correspond to legitimate fine-tuning processes.

The query transcript consistency verification links the entire provenance verification to the specific query-response interaction, ensuring that the proof corresponds to the actual user prompt and generated response rather than being a generic proof that could be reused across different interactions. The verifier checks 
\[
\{0,1\} \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.VERIFY}(pp_{zk}, (C_{m,i}, p, r, \tau), \pi_{\tau})
\]
To confirm that the query transcript \(\tau\) was correctly computed using the committed metadata for the selected dataset, the user's prompt \(p\), and the generated response \(r\). This verification step binds the entire proof to the specific query context, preventing replay attacks where old proofs might be reused for different queries.

The final verification decision aggregates all individual verification results to determine whether the complete provenance chain is valid. If all the verification steps are passed successfully, the $\mathcal{U}$ or any verifier of their choice accepts the response and its claimed provenance. If any verification step fails, the entire proof is rejected, ensuring that partial or incomplete proofs cannot be accepted as valid. The verification process is summarized in Alg.~\ref{alg3}.
\vspace{-3mm}
\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Verification Phase of ZKPROV}
\label{alg3}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{\textsc{Verify}}{$p, r, \pi, C$}
\State \textbf{Input:} Prompt \(p\), Response \(r\), Proof \(\pi\), Commitments \(C\)
%\State \textbf{Output:} \textsc{Accept} or \textsc{Reject}

\State Parse \(\pi \leftarrow (\pi_{\sigma}, \pi_{B}^{rec}, \pi_{\Delta}^{rec}, \pi_{\tau})\)
\State \(\tau \leftarrow \textsc{Hash}(C_{m,i} \parallel p \parallel r \parallel \kappa_3)\)

\State \(\text{public}_{\sigma} \leftarrow (C_{m,i}, C_{\sigma,i}, pk_{\mathcal{CA}})\)
\State \(b_1 \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.VERIFY}(pp_{zk}, \text{public}_{\sigma}, \pi_{\sigma})\)

\State Reconstruct \(S_{B,N}\) from public bindings
\State \(b_2 \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.VERIFY}(pp_{zk}, S_{B,N}, \pi_{B}^{rec})\)

\State Reconstruct \(S_{\Delta,N}\) from public weight consistencies
\State \(b_3 \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.VERIFY}(pp_{zk}, S_{\Delta,N}, \pi_{\Delta}^{rec})\)

\State \(\text{public}_{\tau} \leftarrow (C_{m,i}, p, r, \tau)\)
\State \(b_4 \leftarrow \textsc{ZK.VERIFY}(pp_{zk}, \text{public}_{\tau}, \pi_{\tau})\)

\If{$b_1 = 1 \wedge b_2 = 1 \wedge b_3 = 1 \wedge b_4 = 1$}
    \State \textbf{Return} \textsc{Accept}
\Else
    \State \textbf{Return} \textsc{Reject}
\EndIf
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The proposed design of the ZKPROV ensures that all necessary cryptographic structures are established during the LLM response generation's offline phase, enabling efficient proof generation during query time while maintaining the security properties required for dataset provenance verification. In the next section, we define the security of our proposed scheme and formally prove that ZKPROV binds the model's response to its authorized authenticated dataset.



\section{Security and Privacy Analyses}

\label{sec}


This chapter provides a formal cryptographic analysis of the proposed ZKPROV framework introduced in Section \ref{proposed}. We define the security properties our scheme aims to achieve in Sec. \ref{threat}, and provide its comprehensive proofs of security under standard cryptographic assumptions.

We analyze the security of our proposed scheme for a probabilistic polynomial-time (PPT) adversary $\mathcal{A}$ whose resources, such as time and queries, are bounded by a polynomial in the security parameter $\lambda$. The provider can be a malicious adversary $\mathcal{A}_P$ who has access to a signing oracle $\mathcal{O}_{sign}(\cdot)$, which returns $\sigma_i$. It can query the hash oracle $\mathcal{O}_{hash}(\cdot)$ polynomially many times, may deviate arbitrarily from the protocol specification, and attempts to generate valid proofs for responses derived from unauthorized datasets.

The users are considered semi-honest adversaries $\mathcal{A}_U$ who follow the protocol steps correctly but attempt to learn unauthorized information about dataset contents or model parameters from proofs and public information. Additionally, it has access to all public protocol outputs.

\begin{theorem}[Security of ZKPROV]
\label{thm:main_security_pi}
The ZKPROV provides soundness, dataset exposure binding, and Zero-Knowledge (Privacy) if the underlying KZG commitment scheme is computationally binding and perfectly hiding, the BLS signature scheme is unforgeable, the hash function $\textsc{Hash}$ is collision-resistant, and the HyperNova proof system provides computational soundness and computational zero-knowledge
\end{theorem}

The proof starts by stating that if there exists a PPT adversary $\mathcal{A}$ who can break the soundness and privacy of ZKPROV, we can use this adversary to construct $\mathcal{B}$ to break the corresponding underlying assumption.

We formally define the security experiments and explain the advantage notation used throughout our analysis, where advantages are negligible functions of $\lambda$. Therefore, we have $\Adv^{\text{BLS}}_{\mathcal{B}_1}(\lambda)$: Advantage of adversary $\mathcal{B}_1$ in breaking BLS signature unforgeability, $\Adv^{\text{Bind}}_{\mathcal{B}_2}(\lambda)$: Advantage of adversary $\mathcal{B}_2$ in breaking KZG commitment binding (binding values), $\Adv^{\text{Bind}}_{\mathcal{B}_3}(\lambda)$: Advantage of adversary $\mathcal{B}_3$ in breaking KZG commitment binding (weight consistency), $\Adv^{\text{Hide}}_{\mathcal{B}_6}(\lambda)$: Advantage of adversary $\mathcal{B}_6$ in breaking KZG commitment hiding, $\Adv^{\text{CR}}_{\mathcal{B}_5}(\lambda)$: Advantage of adversary $\mathcal{B}_5$ in finding hash collisions, $\Adv^{\text{HN-Sound}}_{\mathcal{B}_4}(\lambda)$: Advantage of adversary $\mathcal{B}_4$ in breaking HyperNova soundness, and $\Adv^{\text{HN-ZK}}_{\mathcal{B}_7}(\lambda)$: Advantage of adversary $\mathcal{B}_7$ in breaking HyperNova zero-knowledge.


\begin{definition}[Soundness Experiment]
\label{def:soundness_experiment}
The soundness experiment $\Exp^{\text{ZKPROV-Sound}}_{\mathcal{A}_P}(\lambda)$ is defined as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Challenger generates $(\text{crs}, sk_{\mathcal{CA}}, pk_{\mathcal{CA}})$ where $\text{crs}$ contains domain separators $\kappa_1, \kappa_2, \kappa_3$.
    \item Challenger gives $\text{crs}$ and $pk_{\mathcal{CA}}$ to $\mathcal{A}_P$.
    \item Initialize empty query lists $Q_{\mathrm{sign}} = \emptyset$ and $Q_{\mathrm{hash}} = \emptyset$.
    \item $\mathcal{A}_P$ gets access to oracles $\mathcal{O}_{\mathrm{sign}}(\cdot)$ and $\mathcal{O}_{\mathrm{hash}}(\cdot)$.
    \item $\mathcal{A}_P$ outputs $(\mathbf{pub}^*, \pi^*)$.
    \item Return $1$ iff $\textsc{VERIFY}(pp, \mathbf{pub}^*, \pi^*) = \text{accept}$ \\ AND $\mathrm{ForgedProvenance}(\mathcal{A}_P, Q_{\mathrm{sign}}) = 1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

We define $\textsc{ForgedProvenance}(\mathcal{A}_P, Q_{\mathrm{sign}}) = 1$ 
iff $\mathcal{A}_P$'s proof contains a signature on metadata $m^* \notin Q_{\mathrm{sign}}$.
\begin{theorem}[Soundness]
\label{thm:soundness}
For any PPT adversary $\mathcal{A}_P$:
\begin{align}
\Pr[\Exp^{\text{ZKPROV-Sound}}_{\mathcal{A}_P}(\lambda) = 1] \leq &\; \Adv^{\text{BLS}}_{\mathcal{B}_1}(\lambda) + \Adv^{\text{Bind}}_{\mathcal{B}_2}(\lambda) + \Adv^{\text{Bind}}_{\mathcal{B}_3}(\lambda) \nonumber \\
&+ \Adv^{\text{CR}}_{\mathcal{B}_5}(\lambda) + \Adv^{\text{HN-Sound}}_{\mathcal{B}_4}(\lambda) + \frac{N \cdot d}{|\mathbb{F}_p|}
\end{align}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof Sketch]
We construct $\mathcal{B}_1$ that uses HyperNova's knowledge extractor $\mathcal{E}$ to obtain $(m_k, \sigma_k)$. We prove this by analyzing the following four forgery types.

\textbf{Dataset Authentication Forgery:} If $\mathcal{A}_P$ produces a valid proof for metadata $m_k \notin Q_{\mathrm{sign}}$, we construct $\mathcal{B}_1$ that uses HyperNova's knowledge extractor $\mathcal{E}$ to obtain $(m_k, \sigma_k)$ from $\pi_\sigma$ and outputs this as a BLS forgery.

\textbf{Binding Value Forgery:} If $B_{i,j} \neq \langle \Delta W_{i,j}, v_{i,j} \rangle$, either (a) commitments are not binding, so $\mathcal{B}_2$ breaks KZG binding, or (b) distinct polynomials $f_{\text{honest}}(x) = \langle \Delta W_{i,j}, x \rangle$ and $f_{\text{malicious}}(x)$ agree at random challenge $v_{i,j}$. By Schwartz-Zippel, case (b) occurs with probability $\leq d/|\mathbb{F}_p|$ per layer. Challenge unpredictability follows from 
$v_{i,j} = \textsc{Hash}(\kappa_1 \parallel C_{\omega_{\sigma,i}} \parallel C_{\omega_{m,i}} \parallel i \parallel j)$, 
using the commitment hiding of randomness $\omega_{\sigma,i}, \omega_{m,i}$.

\textbf{Weight Consistency Forgery:} If $\Delta W_{i,j} \neq W_{i,j} - W_{0,j}$, but the proof verifies, then either the commitment binding is broken 
($\mathcal{B}_3$ breaks KZG binding) or a false linear relation has a valid proof 
($\mathcal{B}_4$ breaks HyperNova soundness).

\textbf{ Query Transcript Forgery:} If $\tau^* \neq \textsc{Hash}(C_{m_i} \parallel p^* \parallel r^* \parallel \kappa_3)$, but proof passes the verification, $\mathcal{B}_5$ can extract both claimed and correct transcript inputs to find hash collision.

Since breaking the bounds of these defined cases equals breaking the security assumption, we conclude the proof by contradiction.
\end{proof}
\vspace{-2mm}
\begin{definition}[Privacy Experiment]
\label{def:privacy_experiment}
The privacy experiment $\Exp^{\text{ZKPROV-Priv}}_{\mathcal{A}_U}(\lambda)$ is defined as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Challenger generates $(\text{crs}, sk_{\mathcal{CA}}, pk_{\mathcal{CA}})$.
    \item Challenger gives $\text{crs}$ and $pk_{\mathcal{CA}}$ to $\mathcal{A}_U$.
    \item $\mathcal{A}_U$ chooses datasets $D_0, D_1$ with metadata $m_0, m_1$, prompt $p$, hyperparameters $H$, base model $W_0$.
    \item Challenger signs $\sigma_0 \leftarrow \textsc{BLS.Sign}(sk_{\mathcal{CA}}, m_0)$, \\$\sigma_1 \leftarrow \textsc{BLS.Sign}(sk_{\mathcal{CA}}, m_1)$.
    \item Challenger flips $b \stackrel{\$}{\leftarrow} \{0,1\}$.
    \item Challenger computes $W_b \leftarrow \textsc{FINETUNE}(D_b, W_0, H)$, $r_b \leftarrow \textsc{LLM}(W_b, p)$, runs $\textsc{SETUP}$ to generate $C, \Omega$, and \\ $\pi_b \leftarrow \textsc{PROVE}(C, \Omega, p)$.
    \item Challenger gives $(r_b, \pi_b, C)$ to $\mathcal{A}_U$.
    \item $\mathcal{A}_U$ outputs guess $b'$.
    \item Return $1$ iff $b' = b$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Privacy Advantage]
\label{def:privacy_advantage}
The advantage of adversary $\mathcal{A}_U$ in the privacy experiment is:
$$\Adv^{\text{ZKPROV-Priv}}_{\mathcal{A}_U}(\lambda) = \left|\Pr[\Exp^{\text{ZKPROV-Priv}}_{\mathcal{A}_U}(\lambda) = 1] - \frac{1}{2}\right|$$
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-Knowledge]
\label{thm:zero_knowledge}
For any PPT adversary $\mathcal{A}_U$:
$$\Adv^{\text{ZKPROV-Priv}}_{\mathcal{A}_U}(\lambda) \leq \Adv^{\text{Hide}}_{\mathcal{B}_6}(\lambda) + \Adv^{\text{HN-ZK}}_{\mathcal{B}_7}(\lambda)$$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof Sketch]
We prove zero-knowledge via a hybrid argument with three hybrid statements, where each transition is proven computationally indistinguishable based on reductions. We define the simulator $S_{\text{HN}}$ that can generate computationally indistinguishable proofs for the defined statements, and the knowledge extractor $\mathcal{E}$ extracts witnesses from valid proofs with probability $1 - \text{negl}(\lambda)$.

\textbf{Hybrid $H_0$ (Real Experiment):}  
The challenger runs the real privacy experiment where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item All commitments are real: $C_{m_b} = \textsc{C.COMMIT}(m_b, \omega_{m_b})$, $C_{\sigma_b} = \textsc{C.COMMIT}(\sigma_b, \omega_{\sigma_b})$, $C_{\Delta W_b} = \textsc{C.COMMIT}(\Delta W_b, \omega_{\Delta W_b})$, $C_{B_b} = \textsc{C.COMMIT}(B_b, \omega_{B_b})$, $C_{W_0} = \textsc{C.COMMIT}(W_0, \omega_{W_0})$
    \item The proof is real: $\pi_b = (\pi_\sigma, \pi_B^{rec}, \pi_\Delta^{rec}, \pi_\tau) \leftarrow \textsc{PROVE}(C, \Omega, p)$
    \item Output: $(r_b, \pi_b, C)$ where $C = (C_{m_b}, C_{\sigma_b}, C_{\Delta W_b}, C_{B_b}, C_{W_0})$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Hybrid $H_1$ (Simulated Proofs with Real Commitments):}  
The real proofs are replaced with simulated ones. We keep the real commitments:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Keep all real commitments: $C = (C_{m_b}, C_{\sigma_b}, C_{\Delta W_b}, C_{B_b}, C_{W_0})$ as in $H_0$
    \item Generate simulated proof components:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $\tilde{\pi}_\sigma \leftarrow \mathcal{S}_{\text{HN}}(C_{m_b}, C_{\sigma_b}, pk_{\mathcal{CA}})$ 
            \item $\tilde{\pi}_B^{rec} \leftarrow \mathcal{S}_{\text{HN}}$ 
            \item $\tilde{\pi}_\Delta^{rec} \leftarrow \mathcal{S}_{\text{HN}}$ 
            \item $\tilde{\pi}_\tau \leftarrow \mathcal{S}_{\text{HN}}(C_{m_b}, p, r_b, \tau)$ 

        \end{itemize}
    \item Output: $(r_b, \tilde{\pi}_b, C)$ where $\tilde{\pi}_b = (\tilde{\pi}_\sigma, \tilde{\pi}_B^{rec}, \tilde{\pi}_\Delta^{rec}, \tilde{\pi}_\tau)$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Hybrid $H_2$ (Simulated Commitments and Proofs):}  


Replace real commitments with random group elements:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Generate random commitments: $\tilde{C}_{m_b}, \tilde{C}_{\sigma_b}, \tilde{C}_{\Delta W_b}, \tilde{C}_{B_b}, \tilde{C}_{W_0} \stackrel{\$}{\leftarrow} \mathbb{G}_1$
    \item Generate simulated proofs consistent with random commitments: $\tilde{\pi}_b \leftarrow \mathcal{S}_{\text{HN}}(\tilde{C})$
    \item Output: $(r_b, \tilde{\pi}_b, \tilde{C})$ where $\tilde{C} = (\tilde{C}_{m_b}, \tilde{C}_{\sigma_b}, \tilde{C}_{\Delta W_b}, \tilde{C}_{B_b}, \tilde{C}_{W_0})$
\end{itemize}

To analyze the distinguishability argument, we continue as follows.

\textbf{Claim.} $H_0 \approx_c H_1$ with $|\Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_0) = 1] - \Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_1) = 1]| \leq \Adv^{\text{HN-ZK}}_{\mathcal{B}_7}(\lambda)$  

\textit{Proof.} We construct reduction $\mathcal{B}_7$ that uses any distinguisher $\mathcal{D}$ between $H_0$ and $H_1$ to break HyperNova zero-knowledge:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathcal{B}_7$ receives HyperNova public parameters and a statement-proof pair $(stmt, \pi)$ from the HyperNova zero-knowledge challenger, where $\pi$ is either real or simulated.
    \item $\mathcal{B}_7$ simulates the ZKPROV privacy experiment by generating real commitments using actual witnesses.
    \item $\mathcal{B}_7$ embeds the challenge proof $\pi$ as one of the ZKPROV proof components (e.g., $\pi_\sigma$) and generates the remaining components accordingly (real if $\pi$ is real, simulated if $\pi$ is simulated).
    \item $\mathcal{B}_7$ gives the resulting experiment output to $\mathcal{D}$ and forwards $\mathcal{D}$'s output to the HyperNova challenger.
\end{itemize}
The advantage of $\mathcal{B}_7$ in the HyperNova zero-knowledge experiment equals the distinguishing advantage between $H_0$ and $H_1$.

\textbf{Claim.} $H_1 \approx_c H_2$ with $|\Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_1) = 1] - \Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_2) = 1]| \leq \Adv^{\text{Hide}}_{\mathcal{B}_6}(\lambda)$  

\textit{Proof.} We construct adversary $\mathcal{B}_6$ that uses any distinguisher $\mathcal{D}$ between $H_1$ and $H_2$ to break KZG commitment hiding:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathcal{B}_6$ receives KZG public parameters and a commitment $C^*$ from the KZG hiding challenger, where $C^*$ is either a commitment to a real value or a random group element.
    \item $\mathcal{B}_6$ generates the ZKPROV privacy experiment where one of the commitments (e.g., $C_{m_b}$) is set to the challenge commitment $C^*$.
    \item The remaining commitments are generated consistently: real commitments if $C^*$ is real, random group elements if $C^*$ is random.
    \item All proofs are generated using the HyperNova simulator $\mathcal{S}_{\text{HN}}$ to maintain consistency across both cases.
    \item $\mathcal{B}_6$ gives the resulting experiment output to $\mathcal{D}$ and forwards $\mathcal{D}$'s output to the KZG challenger.
\end{itemize}
The advantage of $\mathcal{B}_6$ in the KZG hiding experiment equals the distinguishing advantage between $H_1$ and $H_2$.

\textbf{Claim.} $\Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_2) = 1] = 1/2$  

\textit{Proof.} In hybrid $H_2$, all commitments are random group elements and all proofs are simulated. The only component that depends on the choice bit $b$ is the response $r_b$. However, by the semantic similarity requirement in the privacy experiment (the adversary chooses datasets $D_0, D_1$ that should produce similar responses), and the fact that all cryptographic components are now independent of the actual datasets, the adversary has no advantage in distinguishing between $b = 0$ and $b = 1$.

A direct result of the three claims states that
\begin{align*}
\Adv^{\text{ZKPROV-Priv}}_{\mathcal{A}_U}(\lambda) 
&= \left|\Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_0) = 1] - \frac{1}{2}\right| \\
&= \left|\Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_0) = 1] - \Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_2) = 1]\right| \\
&\leq |\Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_0) = 1] - \Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_1) = 1]| \\
&\quad + |\Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_1) = 1] - \Pr[\mathcal{A}_U(H_2) = 1]| \\
&\leq \Adv^{\text{HN-ZK}}_{\mathcal{B}_7}(\lambda) + \Adv^{\text{Hide}}_{\mathcal{B}_6}(\lambda).
\end{align*}
\textbf{Privacy Implications:} This proof establishes that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dataset Content Privacy:} The weight differences $\Delta W_{i,j}$ that encode dataset-specific information are perfectly hidden by KZG commitments and never revealed through the zero-knowledge proofs.
    \item \textbf{Model Parameter Privacy:} The fine-tuning transformations captured in $\Delta W_{i,j}$ remain confidential, protecting proprietary model improvements.
    \item \textbf{Training Process Privacy:} The binding values $B_{i,j}$ and their computation process remain hidden, preventing adversaries from learning about internal training dynamics.
\end{itemize}
\end{proof}
\vspace{-3mm}
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:main_security_pi}]
The theorem follows directly from Theorems \ref{thm:soundness} and \ref{thm:zero_knowledge}. Since all underlying cryptographic primitives satisfy their respective security properties by assumption, all advantage terms are negligible. The Schwartz-Zippel term $\frac{N \cdot d}{|\mathbb{F}_p|}$ is negligible for appropriately chosen parameters where $|\mathbb{F}_p|$ is exponential in $\lambda$ while $N, d$ are polynomial in $\lambda$. Therefore, ZKPROV achieves both soundness (including dataset exposure binding) and zero-knowledge privacy under the stated cryptographic assumptions.
\end{proof}
\vspace{-5mm}


\section{Evaluation and Comparison}
\label{eval}



This section details the empirical evaluation of the ZKPROV protocol proposed in Section \ref{proposed} and its comparison to the state-of-the-art protocols.

We implemented the complete protocol using a fine-tuned causal language model (\texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B}~\cite{grattafiori2024llama}) from the PubMedQA~\cite{jin2019pubmedqa} dataset to demonstrate its efficacy in proving model provenance with zero-knowledge proofs. Our dataset includes approximately $1,000$ biomedical questions, each paired with curated long-form answers and corresponding binary final decisions (\texttt{yes}/\texttt{no}). Each example is structured with a question \( p \), a set of contextual passages, and a long-form answer, which we denote as \( r \), the LLM's response. Each data sample is preprocessed into a format that combines the question and context as input and concatenates the final decision with the long-form answer as the expected output. The dataset is split into $800$ training samples, $100$ validation samples, and $100$ held-out test samples.

The authorized hyperparameter tuple $H = (\eta, B, E, O)$, where \( \eta = 5 \times 10^{-5} \) is the learning rate, \( B = 8 \) is the effective batch size (accumulated from microbatches of 2), \( E = 3 \) is the number of training epochs, and \( O \) denotes the AdamW optimizer.

Our implementation leverages Microsoft's Nova \cite{kothapalli2022nova} recursive proof system built on a cycle of $BN254$ and Grumpkin elliptic curves with scalar field $\mathbf{F}_p$ where $p \approx 2^{254}$, providing $128$-bit computational security suitable for production deployment. All experiments are conducted on a MacBook Air with an Apple $M4$ chip featuring $10$ cores ($4$ performance and $6$ efficiency cores) and $16$ GB of unified memory.



We evaluated the computational effort of the $\mathcal{P}$ to generate the complete proof $\pi = (\pi_\sigma, \pi_B^{rec}, \pi_\Delta^{rec}, \pi_\tau)$, described in Algorithm \ref{alg2}, and the  $\mathcal{U}$'s effort to verify each component as described in Algorithm \ref{alg3} and summarize them in Table \ref{tab:performance_metrics}.

